; ======================================================================
; display.pi4 - HDMI OUTPUT for the Raspberry Pi 4, through the
;               VideoCore framebuffer. Text first.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; A linear framebuffer is asked for from the VideoCore firmware over the
; mailbox property interface, converted from the bus address the
; firmware hands back into an ARM address, and then written as ordinary
; memory. On top of that sits a fixed-width character cell renderer, a
; scrolling console, and enough of a drawing surface that a real font
; engine can be dropped on later without this file changing.
;
; TEXT IS THE POINT TODAY and graphics are the stated future, so the
; internals are shaped for both: every drawing primitive goes through
; pitch-correct row addressing and a pixel-format dispatch, and the
; glyph data is a separate table taken BY ADDRESS at run time rather
; than compiled into the renderer.
;
; ======================================================================
; USE - in the MAIN file, in this order. Libraries do not include
; libraries (project ruling, 2026-07-30, house rule 10):
;
;     #DSP_DMA_LINKED = 0                           ; MANDATORY, see below
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4"   ; the transport
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/display.pi4"   ; this file
;
; REQUIRES mailbox.pi4, included BEFORE this file. This file includes no
; other file. It calls exactly ONE procedure it does not define, and if
; mailbox.pi4 is missing from the build the compiler stops with a
; SEMANTIC ERROR naming that procedure - which is the house rule working
; and is the correct outcome, not a gap. There is deliberately no stub:
; a stub would let DisplayInit() "succeed" against a framebuffer that
; was never allocated, and the failure would surface as a dead monitor
; rather than as a missing include.
;
; ALSO REQUIRES ONE CONSTANT, #DSP_DMA_LINKED, DEFINED BY THE MAIN FILE
; BEFORE THIS ONE. 0 means the CPU path and nothing else; 1 means
; RaspberryPi4/Lib/dma.pi4 is in the build ahead of this file and the
; hardware blitter may be used. There is no default and there cannot be
; one - the reasoning, and the whole cache analysis that goes with it,
; is in THE DMA SEAM and CACHE COHERENCY WITH A BUS MASTER below.
;
; WHAT #DSP_DMA_LINKED = 0 ACTUALLY COSTS, measured rather than claimed:
; every procedure that touches the engine is absent from the image - a
; dead CompilerIf branch is skipped at the token level and never parsed
; - and what remains is TEN ACCESSORS SPANNING 660 BYTES and six cells
; of BSS. Counted out of pi4FbRaw.img.sym on 2026-08-27:
; displaydmalinked at $8B60 through displaydmastatsreset, with
; dsprowfill32 the next symbol at $8DF4. Nothing named DspDma* or
; DmaCache* appears at all. The accessors are outside the CompilerIf on
; purpose and the reason is at their declaration: a program must be able
; to ask "was the blitter linked?" and be told no, rather than fail to
; compile the question.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE MAILBOX CONTRACT - RECONCILED AGAINST THE REAL mailbox.pi4
; ======================================================================
; This file was drafted against an ASSUMED transport, because
; RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4 did not exist when it was started. It
; landed part-way through and this file was rewritten onto the real one.
; The assumption is recorded here only so the difference is on the
; record rather than silently smoothed over:
;
;   ASSUMED   MailboxPropertyCall(*buf) - display.pi4 builds a whole
;             multi-tag property buffer and hands the transport a
;             pointer to it.
;   ACTUAL    mailbox.pi4 OWNS the buffer. It was strictly SINGLE-TAG
;             until 2026-08-26 and it composes now, but the ownership
;             never moved: this file supplies tag numbers and words and
;             reads answers back, and there is no property-buffer memory
;             here.
;
; THE ENTRY POINTS THIS FILE USES, and they are the whole of its
; dependency:
;
;     MailboxInit()                        once, before anything
;     MailboxReady()                       1 when init succeeded
;     MailboxError()                       why the last one failed
;   one tag, one message - used for the panel query only
;     MailboxBegin(tag, reqB, respB)       lay out one tag
;     MailboxSend()                        send, and validate the reply
;     MailboxWord(i)                       read response word i
;   several tags, one message - used for everything else
;     MailboxListBegin()                   start composing
;     MailboxListAdd(tag, reqB, respB)     append a tag, get its index
;     MailboxListSetWord(t, i, v)          fill request word i of tag t
;     MailboxListSend()                    send once, validate every tag
;     MailboxListWord(t, i)                read response word i of tag t
;     MailboxListCount()                   how many went in
;     MailboxListFailedTag()               which one was not answered
;
; ======================================================================
; ONE TAG PER MESSAGE WAS WRONG. WHAT IT COST, AND HOW IT WAS FOUND.
; ======================================================================
; THIS SECTION REPLACES A PARAGRAPH THAT ARGUED THE OPPOSITE, and the
; argument is kept in full because it was not careless - it was
; carefully wrong, which is the kind that survives review.
;
; WHAT IT SAID: "ONE TAG PER MESSAGE IS NOT A COMPROMISE FORCED BY THAT
; API... the widely copied bare-metal recipe puts all seven framebuffer
; tags in one message. The kernel does not:
; Datasheets/pi4/raspberrypi-firmware.c:155-176, rpi_firmware_property(),
; builds a list containing exactly ONE tag and hands it to
; rpi_firmware_property_list(). Single-tag messages are the mainline
; upstream path, so the sequence below is the well-trodden one and the
; all-in-one message is the shortcut." It went on to name atomicity as
; the only thing given up, and to note that nothing reconfigures the
; display from two places at once, so nothing would notice.
;
; EVERY FACT IN THAT PARAGRAPH IS TRUE. The kernel really does send one
; tag per message, and rpi_firmware_property() really is the mainline
; path. The inference from those facts is what was wrong, in two places:
;
;   * The kernel's single-tag helper is a CONVENIENCE WRAPPER around
;     rpi_firmware_property_list(), which exists precisely because
;     several tags in one buffer is a real and supported thing. The
;     kernel's own framebuffer driver does not use the wrapper.
;   * Atomicity is not the only thing at stake. For the FRAMEBUFFER
;     tags, one transaction is not a nicety - it is the mechanism. The
;     firmware accumulates the requested framebuffer state WITHIN A
;     SINGLE PROPERTY TRANSACTION and allocates against what that
;     transaction accumulated. Separate messages do not build on one
;     another. The allocate ran against a default.
;
; WHAT IT COST. Booted through U-Boot, the seven-message sequence worked
; perfectly, every time, for as long as it existed - because U-Boot had
; already made the combined call and the firmware had a real mode
; configured before our first message went out. Booted STRAIGHT FROM THE
; FIRMWARE, DisplayInit() returned #DSP_OK and handed back a 2 x 2
; framebuffer with a pitch of 64, in front of a 1920 x 1080 panel that a
; get-physical query described correctly a moment later. The console
; built on that surface computed a NEGATIVE number of rows, which is how
; anyone noticed at all.
;
; THE WRONG THEORY, AND HOW IT DIED. The first explanation was timing -
; "the firmware has not settled on a mode yet at the instant it is
; asked" - and this file carried a retry written against it, with a
; comment saying so. RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/pi4FbSettle.pi4
; was written to test exactly that and nothing else: ask, wait, ask
; again, ten times over 1.8 seconds. It returned 2 x 2 on every single
; attempt. A request that is shaped wrong does not become right by being
; repeated, and the retry is gone.
;
; THE AUTHORITY, on this disk since the investigation:
;   Datasheets/pi4/uboot/v2025.01_msg.c   - U-Boot v2025.01,
;     arch/arm/mach-bcm283x/msg.c. :29-33 struct msg_query, one tag.
;     :35-47 struct msg_setup, NINE tags in one buffer. :140-157
;     bcm2835_get_video_size() sends the query. :159-204
;     bcm2835_set_video_params() fills all nine, makes ONE call, and
;     reads the size, the pitch, the framebuffer address and the
;     framebuffer size back out of that same response.
;   Datasheets/pi4/uboot/v2025.01_mbox.h  - the tag numbers, the
;     request and response structs, and at :31-36 the sentence in prose:
;     "With some limitations, an arbitrary set of tags may be combined
;     together into a single message buffer."
;
; WHAT THE OLD DESIGN GOT RIGHT AND THE NEW ONE KEEPS: the firmware
; CLAMPS a request it cannot satisfy and reports success. The width,
; height and depth this file reports are the ones the firmware echoed,
; never the ones that were asked for. That was true when the echo came
; back in seven separate replies and it is true now that all of it comes
; back in one; the difference is only that there is no longer a window
; between the reply and the next request for the state to be observed
; half-configured in.
;
; ONE MORE THING THE OLD SHAPE HID. Reading the pitch out of a SECOND
; message asks a different transaction for a property of a surface the
; first one created. It happened to work under U-Boot for the same
; reason everything else did.
;
; WHAT mailbox.pi4 ALREADY CHECKS, so this file does not repeat it: the
; bus-address conversion and its 1 GiB limit, the 16-byte alignment,
; cache maintenance on the buffer either way, channel 8 in the low
; nibble, bounded waits on both mailboxes, that the reply names OUR
; buffer, that the response code is SUCCESS, and - the one that matters
; most here - that the firmware SET THE TAG'S OWN RESPONSE BIT. That
; last check is what turns a wrong tag number from silent zeros into a
; loud #MBX_ERR_TAG, and it is the reason the tag slots below are merely
; unverified rather than dangerous.
;
; THE TRANSPORT NUMBERS ARE NOT DUPLICATED HERE. They belong to
; mailbox.pi4, whose header cites all of them to files on this disk, and
; two copies of a register address is one copy too many.
;
; ONE CORRECTION THIS FILE OWES mailbox.pi4: an earlier draft of this
; header cited the BCM2711 peripheral manual's chapter 13, "ARM
; Mailboxes", as the manual section for this device. THAT IS THE WRONG
; DEVICE - chapter 13 describes the sixteen core-to-core mailboxes in
; the ARM_LOCAL block, not the VideoCore property channel, and grepping
; the manual for "b880" returns nothing. The claim is withdrawn rather
; than restated. The vault's scoping note carries the same conflation
; and should be corrected there too.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE TAG NUMBERS ARE NOT CITED, AND THIS FILE WILL NOT GUESS THEM
; ======================================================================
; Every numeric property tag - and the two status codes - lives in
; enum rpi_firmware_property_tag in that same missing kernel header.
; A grep of the WHOLE of the private development workspace for 0x00040001, 0x00048003,
; 0x00040008, RPI_FIRMWARE_FRAMEBUFFER*, FRAMEBUFFER_ALLOCATE and
; SET_PHYSICAL_WIDTH returns nothing. raspberrypi-firmware.c references
; the enum by NAME only; no numeric value of any tag appears anywhere on
; this disk.
;
; The vault already knew this and had already flagged it:
;   "Raspberry Pi 4/MAP.md" lines 73-76 - "Still unverified, and both
;   are firmware documentation rather than this manual: the kernel load
;   address and boot handoff detail, and the framebuffer property-tag
;   set."
;   "Raspberry Pi 4/Scoping - Raspberry Pi 4 bare metal backend.md"
;   lines 113-114 - "Mailbox tags to verify against the firmware wiki:
;   allocate buffer, set physical/virtual size, set depth, get pitch."
;   Names. No numbers. It has been an open item since the target was
;   scoped.
;
; mailbox.pi4 REACHED THE SAME CONCLUSION INDEPENDENTLY and acted on it.
; Its header lists the framebuffer family under "DELIBERATELY LEFT OUT":
;
;   "the FRAMEBUFFER tag family ($00040001 and its neighbours). The
;    vault already flags the framebuffer property-tag set as
;    'unverified, firmware documentation rather than the manual', and
;    that family is not a handful of numbers but a whole protocol -
;    allocate, pitch, depth, virtual offset, and a two-pass sequence
;    where the firmware rewrites your request. It deserves its own
;    library, written against a display on the bench, not a guess
;    appended to this one."
;
; That library is this one, and it inherits the same refusal to guess.
; The tags mailbox.pi4 DOES implement (clocks, temperature, board
; revision) it took from the kernel header and cross-checked against the
; firmware project's own documentation, two independent sources agreeing
; value by value. That is a defensible bar, but both sources are OFF
; THIS MACHINE, and the instruction this file was written under names
; the on-disk sources specifically. So:
;
;   THE SEVEN FRAMEBUFFER TAG NUMBERS ARE NOT WRITTEN DOWN IN THIS FILE.
;   They are seven empty slots. DisplayInit() refuses with #DSP_ENOTAGS
;   until the five required ones are filled in by the caller, and names
;   the first one still missing through DisplayMissingSlot().
;
;   UPDATE 2026-08-25 - THE NUMBERS ARE NOW ON DISK, AND THIS DESIGN
;   STAYS ANYWAY. The download was authorised; the kernel header is at
;   Datasheets/pi4/raspberrypi-firmware.h and all seven values live in
;   mailbox.pi4 as #MBX_TAG_FB_*, each with its line number. Call
;   DisplayStandardTags() to fill the slots from them.
;
;   IT FILLED SIX OF THE SEVEN, leaving set-pixel-order empty because
;   the tag number had become citable and the meaning of its VALUE had
;   not.
;
;   UPDATE 2026-08-26 - TEN SLOTS, ALL FILLED, ALL REQUIRED. The
;   combined message needs three tags this file did not have - alpha
;   mode, overscan, and the get-physical query that runs before it - and
;   it needs the pixel order that used to be skipped. The pixel-order
;   value stopped being uncitable the same day, because U-Boot's mailbox
;   header arrived with the investigation:
;   Datasheets/pi4/uboot/v2025.01_mbox.h:373-374. The long note on
;   DisplayStandardTags() argues that reversal properly; it is not
;   summarised twice.
;
;   What did NOT change: DisplayInit() still refuses by default. The
;   slots were never a workaround for missing paperwork - they are how a
;   caller states which firmware interface it is talking to. A default
;   that quietly assumed one would be the same mistake the tag numbers
;   were being careful about, one level up.
;
; The slots. The NAME of each is from raspberrypi-firmware.c and from
; the firmware property-interface documentation; only the VALUE is what
; could not be read off this disk:
;
;   #DSP_TAG_SETPHYS      set physical width and height
;   #DSP_TAG_SETVIRT      set virtual width and height
;   #DSP_TAG_SETDEPTH     set bits per pixel
;   #DSP_TAG_ALLOCATE     allocate buffer
;   #DSP_TAG_GETPITCH     get pitch
;   #DSP_TAG_SETORDER     set pixel order
;   #DSP_TAG_SETOFFSET    set virtual offset
;   #DSP_TAG_SETALPHA     set alpha mode          added 2026-08-26
;   #DSP_TAG_SETOVERSCAN  set overscan            added 2026-08-26
;   #DSP_TAG_GETPHYS      get physical w and h    added 2026-08-26
;
; The envelope constants an earlier draft also had slots for - the
; request code, the success code and the end tag - ARE GONE FROM HERE.
; mailbox.pi4 owns the envelope and defines all three itself
; (#MBX_REQUEST, #MBX_SUCCESS, #MBX_END_TAG). Keeping duplicates would
; have been two places to get the same number wrong.
;
; TWO OF THEM USED TO BE OPTIONAL AND SKIPPED IF UNSET. That is over,
; and the reason it is over is the reason the whole file changed. This
; header used to say: "unset means 'leave the firmware's own default
; alone', which for pixel order and virtual offset is a defensible
; answer and for the other five is not." It was defensible while each
; tag was its own message. Inside ONE transaction, from which the
; firmware allocates, an omitted tag is not a default left alone - it is
; a gap in the state the allocate reads. All ten are required now and
; DisplayStandardTags() fills all ten.
;
; Fill them in once, before DisplayInit():
;
;     DisplaySetTag(#DSP_TAG_SETPHYS,  <value>)
;     DisplaySetTag(#DSP_TAG_SETVIRT,  <value>)
;     ...
;
; WHEN SOMEBODY VERIFIES THEM AGAINST A REAL DISPLAY: put them in the
; main program, or in mailbox.pi4 beside the tags it already carries,
; and leave this file's slots as slots. Being correct about what it does
; not know is this file's job; being the home of a number whose
; provenance is a wiki page is not.
;
; TWO BACKSTOPS, because "the caller supplied the numbers" is not the
; same as "the numbers were right":
;
;   1. THE TRANSPORT CHECKS EVERY TAG'S OWN RESPONSE BIT, not just the
;      whole-buffer success code. mailbox.pi4's header is explicit about
;      why: "a whole-buffer SUCCESS is not the same claim as 'this tag
;      was understood'. The firmware marks each tag it answered by
;      setting bit 31 of the tag's own code word; a tag it did not
;      recognise is left clear while the buffer still reports success."
;      That check matters MORE now that nine tags travel together, not
;      less: eight understood and the ninth not is a buffer that reports
;      success over a display eight tags already reconfigured.
;      MailboxListSend() walks all nine and MailboxListFailedTag() names
;      the first one left clear, so a wrong tag number still surfaces as
;      the #DSP_E... code for exactly that tag - not a generic failure
;      and never a plausible zero.
;   2. DisplayInit() then validates the fields it was given as numbers:
;      a non-zero base, a non-zero pitch, a pitch at least
;      width*bytes-per-pixel, an allocated size at least pitch*height,
;      and - added 2026-08-26 - a surface big enough to hold a single
;      character cell, which is the backstop for the 2 x 2 answer that
;      started all this. Arithmetic, not magic numbers.
;
; ======================================================================
; PITCH IS NOT WIDTH * BYTES-PER-PIXEL, AND THAT IS THE CLASSIC BUG
; ======================================================================
; The firmware returns the pitch - the number of BYTES from the start of
; one row to the start of the next - as a separate tag, and it is free
; to be larger than width*bpp. It usually is: the VideoCore pads rows to
; a hardware-friendly boundary, so a 1920-wide 32bpp surface can come
; back with a pitch of 7680 (exact) or of something larger, and 1366x768
; - a very common HDMI mode - is the case that bites, because 1366*4 is
; 5464 and the firmware will hand back 5504 or similar.
;
; Using width*bpp where the pitch belongs does not produce a blank
; screen or a crash. It produces a picture that SHEARS DIAGONALLY: each
; row starts a few pixels earlier than the one above it, so the image
; leans, and text turns into a staircase. It looks like a mode problem
; or a bad cable, which is why it costs an afternoon.
;
; So: dsp_pitch is read from the firmware, EVERY row address in this
; file is computed as base + y*dsp_pitch + x*bpp, and NOTHING here
; multiplies width by bytes-per-pixel to find a row. The only place
; width*bpp appears at all is the sanity check that refuses a pitch
; SMALLER than width*bpp, which would be the firmware contradicting
; itself.
;
; DisplayPitch() reports it. If it ever equals width*bpp exactly, that
; is a coincidence of the mode, not a rule to start relying on.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE FRAMEBUFFER POINTER IS A BUS ADDRESS - the $C0000000 question
; ======================================================================
; The allocate-buffer tag returns a VIDEOCORE BUS address, not an ARM
; physical one. The VideoCore sees the first gigabyte of DRAM through
; four aliases that differ only in bits 31:30, which select the cache
; behaviour of the VPU's own accesses; the low 30 bits are the same
; offset into DRAM in all four.
;
; The mapping is on disk, in the device tree, and it is the SAME fact
; the mailbox transport needs for the request buffer going the other
; way:
;
;     Datasheets/pi4/bcm2711.dtsi:45
;       dma-ranges = <0xc0000000  0x0 0x00000000  0x40000000>;
;
; Read as <bus-address, parent-address-hi, parent-address-lo, size>:
; bus $C0000000 is ARM physical $00000000 for $40000000 bytes. So
;
;     arm = bus & $3FFFFFFF
;
; which clears the two alias-selector bits and keeps the offset. This
; file uses that, in DisplayInit(), once, and stores only the ARM
; address afterwards.
;
; TWO THINGS THAT COULD MAKE THAT MASK WRONG, both stated rather than
; assumed away:
;   * It is correct only while the allocation lands in the low gigabyte
;     that dma-ranges covers. On a Pi 4 the firmware allocates the
;     framebuffer out of the VideoCore's own gpu_mem split, which is at
;     the top of the low 1 GiB, so it does. If a future firmware were to
;     place it higher, this mask would silently fold the address back
;     into the low gigabyte - so DisplayInit() checks that the result is
;     non-zero and that the reported size is big enough, and a caller
;     that wants certainty can read DisplayBusBase() and see the raw
;     value the firmware actually returned.
;   * The response word is read out of a 32-bit slot. On this target a
;     PeekL SIGN-EXTENDS into the X register, and a bus address of
;     $DE400000 has bit 31 set, so it would arrive as a NEGATIVE 64-bit
;     value and "bus & $3FFFFFFF" would still be right by accident while
;     "bus - $C0000000" would not. Every 32-bit field read in this file
;     is masked with & $FFFFFFFF the moment it is read. That masking is
;     MailboxWord()'s, not this file's - its own comment gives the same
;     reason. See the 64-bit section below.
;
; ======================================================================
; 64-BIT: WHICH ACCESS WIDTH, EVERYWHERE, AND WHY
; ======================================================================
; On this target .i, pointers and every integer intermediate are EIGHT
; bytes, so a bare Peek/Poke is a 64-bit access. Nothing in this file
; uses a bare one. Every access says its width on purpose:
;
;   PeekL / PokeL   32-bit.  Every pixel at 32bpp. (The property buffer
;                   is not this file's any more - mailbox.pi4 pokes it,
;                   and does so 32 bits at a time for the same reason.)
;   PeekW / PokeW   16-bit.  Every pixel at 16bpp.
;   PeekB           8-bit.   Glyph bitmap rows, and string characters.
;
; A bare Poke into the framebuffer would write EIGHT bytes - two pixels
; at 32bpp - and the last one of a row would run off the end of the
; surface. It would not fault; it would be silently wrong, which is the
; whole reason the widths are spelled out.
;
; THREE 64-BIT TRAPS THIS FILE IS WRITTEN AROUND:
;
;   1. PeekL SIGN-EXTENDS. Any 32-bit field with bit 31 set comes back
;      negative. Mailbox response words are masked by MailboxWord()
;      before this file ever sees them; a pixel read back through
;      DisplayGetPixel() is masked here, for the same reason - an opaque
;      white pixel has its top alpha byte set and so has bit 31 set.
;
;   2. A 32-BIT MASK IS NOT AN ALIGNMENT MASK ANY MORE. "a & $FFFFFFF0"
;      to 16-byte-align an address clears the entire top half of a
;      64-bit address as well as the low four bits. Where this file
;      aligns, it SHIFTS - ((a + 15) >> 4) << 4 - which is the same
;      lesson the monitor's CacheFlushRange() records at its own site.
;
;   3. OPERATOR PRECEDENCE IS NOT WHAT THE PARENT DIALECT DOES: the
;      bitwise operators & | ! bind TIGHTER than the shifts << >>.
;      So "flags | 1 << n" parses as "(flags | 1) << n" and
;      "v >> 8 & 255" parses as "v >> (8 & 255)". EVERY shift in this
;      file is parenthesised, without exception, including the ones
;      where it would not have mattered.
;
; ======================================================================
; CACHE COHERENCY - the thing a framebuffer needs and an SPI panel does not
; ======================================================================
; An image entered through the monitor or through U-Boot's "go" starts
; at EL2 WITH THE MMU AND CACHES ON and U-Boot's page tables live, which
; is recorded from the bench in the vault's "RAM execution plan -
; U-Boot first 2026-08-24.md". DRAM is mapped normal cacheable there.
;
; That means a PokeL into the framebuffer can sit in the D-cache and
; never reach the memory the display controller is scanning out of. The
; screen shows stale pixels, or nothing, and the code looks right.
;
; So this file cleans what it draws:
;
;   DisplayFlushRect(x, y, w, h)   dc cvac over the bytes that rectangle
;                                  covers, one 64-byte line at a time,
;                                  then dsb sy
;   DisplayFlush()                 the whole surface
;
; cvac - clean to the Point of Coherency - is the right operation: the
; reader is the display controller, not the instruction fetcher, so this
; is the DMA case, not the self-modifying-code case that wants cvau.
; (The monitor uses cvau for exactly that other reason, and its header
; is where the encoding table for these lives. dc cvac, x9 assembles to
; $D50B7A29, verified against the emitted image, not from the table.)
;
; 64 bytes is the Cortex-A72 cache line, the same constant and the same
; reasoning as anvil.pi4's CacheLine().
;
; IF THE MMU IS OFF the flush is unnecessary and harmless - every access
; is already Device memory and dc cvac on an unmapped-as-cacheable line
; is a no-op. So the same code is correct in both worlds and there is no
; mode to configure.
;
; AUTO-FLUSH IS ON BY DEFAULT, because "loud correct over silent wrong"
; is worth more here than speed: a library that draws into a cache and
; shows nothing is indistinguishable from a dead HDMI port. Every
; primitive flushes the rectangle it touched. That is one dc per 64
; bytes, which is fine for text and wasteful for a per-pixel loop, so:
;
;     DisplayAutoFlush(0)     ... draw a lot ...     DisplayFlush()
;     DisplayAutoFlush(1)
;
; is the documented escape, and it is the caller's deliberate choice
; rather than a default that quietly loses pixels.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE DMA SEAM - #DSP_DMA_LINKED, AND THE ONE PARAGRAPH TO READ FIRST
; ======================================================================
; RaspberryPi4/Lib/dma.pi4 drives the SoC's legacy 2D blitter. It can
; fill a rectangle and copy a rectangle without the CPU touching a
; single pixel. This file can hand it the console's rectangle fills and
; the console's scroll - BUT ONLY IF THE MAIN PROGRAM ASKS FOR IT, and
; the asking is a compile-time constant because this dialect has
; neither procedure pointers (uart.pi4:468-470 records that finding)
; nor a way to test whether a constant is defined.
;
; THE MAIN FILE MUST DEFINE #DSP_DMA_LINKED BEFORE INCLUDING THIS FILE.
; There is no default and there cannot be one: a default here and a
; different value in the main is a hard "Constant already defined"
; error (ParserCore.pbi:4937-4941), so the choice belongs to the main
; and to nowhere else. This is x509.pico2's #X509_SIG_LINKED seam
; (x509.pico2:50-58) applied unchanged, and for the same reason.
;
;   #DSP_DMA_LINKED = 0     ; the CPU path, and nothing else exists
;   XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4"
;   XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/display.pi4"
;
;   #DSP_DMA_LINKED = 1     ; dma.pi4 is in the build, BEFORE this file
;   XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4"
;   XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/dma.pi4"
;   XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/display.pi4"
;
; WITH IT AT 0 NOT ONE INSTRUCTION OF THE ENGINE PATH IS EMITTED. A
; dead CompilerIf branch is skipped at the token level and never parsed
; (ParserCore.pbi:2395-2426, the FE2-3 fix), so a program that does not
; want the blitter does not carry one byte of it, does not gain a
; dependency, and cannot be broken by a defect in it. What it DOES
; carry is the ten accessors - 660 bytes, measured, see the USE section
; - which are outside the seam so that a program can always ask what it
; got. That property is
; the whole reason the seam is compile-time rather than a runtime test
; of a flag: a runtime test would drag dma.pi4's 2300 lines into the
; monitor, which brings a screen up long before there is any question
; of accelerating it, and this backend does not dead-strip
; (math.pi4:127-132).
;
; AND WITH IT AT 1 THE ENGINE IS STILL OFF UNTIL DisplayUseDma(1).
; Two switches, not one, and the second is deliberate: linking the
; library is a build decision and using it is a run decision, and a
; bring-up needs to be able to turn the engine off at the bench without
; a rebuild to find out which side of the seam a wrong picture came
; from. uart.pi4's mirror is off by default for the same reason and
; says so at :485-489.
;
; ======================================================================
; CACHE COHERENCY WITH A BUS MASTER. THIS IS THE SECTION THAT MATTERS.
; ======================================================================
; The section above is about ONE non-coherent reader - the display
; controller - and one direction, and it is solved by a clean. The DMA
; engine is a non-coherent reader AND a non-coherent WRITER, which is a
; strictly harder problem with four distinct hazards, and only one of
; them is the one people think of.
;
; ---- THE GROUND TRUTH, CITED ----------------------------------------
;
;   * DRAM IS CACHEABLE. mmu.pi4:1001 sets #MMU_DRAM_ATTR = $711: a
;     block descriptor with memory-type index 4, inner-shareable, access
;     flag set (mmu.pi4:967-983). MAIR index 4 is $FF - Normal, Inner
;     and Outer Write-Back Non-transient, Read- and Write-Allocate
;     (mmu.pi4:907-919). Write-back AND read-allocate: a line can be
;     dirty, and a line can be pulled in by a READ that never wrote
;     anything.
;
;   * THE FRAMEBUFFER IS INSIDE THAT MAPPING. dsp_base is
;     bus & $3FFFFFFF (see the address conversion in DisplayInit), so
;     the surface is in the low gigabyte, which mmu.pi4:1122-1124 maps
;     with #MMU_DRAM_ATTR through level-1 slot 0. The framebuffer is
;     ordinary cacheable DRAM as far as the CPU is concerned. It is not
;     special, it is not Device, and nothing about it is write-through.
;
;   * THE ENGINE IS NOT COHERENT. It is a bus master on the AXI fabric
;     with no snoop path into the A72's caches, and it addresses DRAM
;     through the VideoCore's UNCACHED alias at $C0000000
;     (dma.pi4:1004-1024). Its reads see DRAM. Its writes go to DRAM.
;     Neither sees or disturbs a cache line.
;
; ---- HAZARD A: WHAT THE ENGINE READS ---------------------------------
;   The CPU wrote something and it is sitting in a dirty line. The
;   engine reads DRAM and gets what DRAM last saw, which is usually
;   whatever was there before.
;
;   dma.pi4 handles its OWN two cases - the control block and the fill
;   pattern - on every single transfer (dma.pi4:1519-1530). It does not
;   handle the caller's source rectangle and says so.
;
;   FOR THE SCROLL THE SOURCE IS THIS SURFACE, so it is ours. It must
;   be cleaned before the engine reads it, AND THAT IS TRUE EVEN WHEN
;   AUTO-FLUSH IS OFF. That last clause is the trap. Today dsp_auto
;   decides only WHEN the display controller sees a pixel, so turning
;   it off costs nothing but a late repaint; the moment the engine is
;   in the path, dsp_auto = 0 with no other clean means the SCROLL
;   COPIES STALE PIXELS. The two settings are not independent any more
;   and the DMA path must not consult dsp_auto at all.
;
; ---- HAZARD B: WHAT THE CPU READS AFTERWARDS -------------------------
;   The engine wrote DRAM. The CPU may hold a stale but CLEAN line over
;   those addresses - from a DisplayGetPixel, from the glyph renderer,
;   from a previous DisplayCopyRect, or from nothing the program did at
;   all, because Normal memory may be speculatively read at any time
;   and this part read-allocates. Every later CPU read of that
;   rectangle returns the old picture.
;
;   So the destination must be INVALIDATED AFTER the transfer, not
;   merely before it. Cleaning before is not sufficient for this one:
;   the window between the pre-pass and the CPU's read is exactly where
;   a prefetch lands.
;
; ---- HAZARD C: THE ONE THAT BITES LATER AND IN THE WRONG PLACE -------
;   The CPU drew into this rectangle earlier with the caches on and
;   those lines are STILL DIRTY. The engine writes DRAM. Some time
;   later - a microsecond, a frame, a second - the cache evicts those
;   dirty lines and writes the OLD pixels back over the new ones.
;
;   THIS IS THE WORST OF THE FOUR AND IT IS THE LEAST OBVIOUS:
;     * it corrupts whole 64-byte lines, so it looks like blocky
;       garbage, which reads as a DMA fault rather than a cache fault;
;     * it happens AFTER the transfer completed successfully, so every
;       status bit says the transfer was fine, because it was;
;     * it is non-deterministic - it depends on cache pressure and the
;       replacement policy - so it will not reproduce on a small test
;       and it will reproduce on a busy screen;
;     * and it gets worse the FASTER the rest of the program is,
;       because more pressure means earlier eviction.
;
;   The fix is a CLEAN AND INVALIDATE of the destination BEFORE the
;   transfer. Clean, so any dirty line is written back now, while the
;   engine has not yet overwritten it; invalidate, so the line is gone
;   and cannot be evicted later.
;
; ---- HAZARD D: THE LINE IS BIGGER THAN THE RECTANGLE -----------------
;   A rectangle edge is not cache-line aligned. A 64-byte line at the
;   left or right edge of the rectangle holds bytes that belong to the
;   rectangle AND bytes that do not. That is fine for a synchronous
;   blitter, because the CPU is blocked inside DmaWait() and cannot be
;   writing those neighbouring bytes.
;
;   IT IS NOT FINE FOR AN OVERLAPPED ONE, and DmaBusy()/DmaWait() are
;   public precisely so an overlapped variant can be written
;   (dma.pi4:291-297). Written down now, before there is a caller:
;
;     WHILE A TRANSFER IS IN FLIGHT THE CPU MUST NOT WRITE ANY CACHE
;     LINE THAT THE DESTINATION RECTANGLE TOUCHES - INCLUDING BYTES
;     OUTSIDE THE RECTANGLE BUT INSIDE ONE OF ITS EDGE LINES.
;
;   "Outside the rectangle" and "outside the affected lines" are
;   different regions and the difference is up to 63 bytes on each edge
;   of each row.
;
; ---- WHAT THIS FILE THEREFORE DOES, IN ORDER -------------------------
;   Every DMA-accelerated primitive here runs exactly this sequence,
;   and it is in ONE procedure - DspDmaSyncRect - so there is one place
;   for it to be wrong:
;
;     1. DspDmaSyncRect over the WHOLE region the transfer touches -
;        for a fill that is the destination; for a copy or a scroll it
;        is the UNION of source and destination, which for an
;        overlapping scroll is one contiguous range and one pass rather
;        than two. This is "dc civac": clean (hazards A and C) and
;        invalidate (part of B) in one instruction.
;     2. the transfer, through dma.pi4, which cleans its own control
;        block and pattern and waits with CS.WAIT_FOR_OUTSTANDING_WRITES
;        set, so completion means the bytes are in DRAM.
;     3. DspDmaSyncRect over the destination again, IF the CPU may read
;        those pixels back. Hazard B. It is a knob - DisplayDmaReadback -
;        and it DEFAULTS ON, on this file's standing principle that loud
;        correct beats silent wrong; a program that provably never reads
;        the surface back can turn it off and save one civac per 64
;        bytes.
;     4. NO DspTouched. This is not an oversight and it must not be
;        "fixed". The engine wrote DRAM directly; the pixels are already
;        where the display controller reads them. A clean afterwards
;        would at best be 130,000 wasted operations and at worst - if
;        step 1 had been skipped - would write a stale line back over a
;        correct transfer. The auto-flush belongs to the CPU path only.
;
; ---- WHY THE ANSWER IS NOT "MAP IT NON-CACHEABLE" --------------------
;   It would remove all four hazards and every maintenance instruction,
;   so it deserves an answer rather than a dismissal. Three costs, and
;   the third is decisive:
;
;     1. THE CPU PATH GETS SLOWER AND IT IS THE REFERENCE. Measured on
;        this board, 2026-08-26, full surface (damage.pi4:43-47):
;        the clear went 356.9 ms uncached to 266.4 ms cached, and the
;        READ side went 1787.0 ms to 1155.0 ms. The CPU path is not
;        going away - it is what the DMA path is checked against - so
;        making it 1.3x to 1.55x slower to simplify the accelerated
;        path is paying in the wrong currency.
;
;     2. THE MAINTENANCE IS NOT THE EXPENSIVE PART ANYWAY. One civac
;        per 64 bytes over 8.29 MB is 129,600 operations. Against a CPU
;        fill of 266.4 ms that is noise, and against a DMA fill it is
;        the entire remaining cost - which is exactly why it is timed
;        and printed separately by pi4DmaProbe.pi4 rather than hidden.
;
;     3. THE GRANULARITY IS 2 MiB AND THE FRAMEBUFFER IS NOT ALONE IN
;        IT. mmu.pi4 maps the low gigabyte with 1 GiB blocks
;        (mmu.pi4:1116-1124) and only the top gigabyte in 2 MiB blocks
;        (mmu.pi4:1099-1112). Making the framebuffer non-cacheable means
;        splitting slot 0 and mapping at least a 2 MiB block - and in
;        practice the surrounding megabytes - as Normal-NC. That is a
;        change to mmu.pi4's map, which every other driver's cache
;        discipline is currently being validated against, to buy a
;        simplification in one file.
;
;   THE ONE PLACE NON-CACHEABLE WOULD GENUINELY BE RIGHT is dma.pi4's
;   512-byte scratch block, which the CPU writes and the engine reads on
;   every single transfer and nothing ever reads back. It is refused for
;   reason 3 alone: 512 bytes cannot be mapped differently from the
;   2 MiB around them, and the 2 MiB around them is payload.
;
; ---- AND A GATE WITH A FLAT MEMORY MODEL CANNOT CATCH ANY OF THIS ----
;   Said plainly because it is the most important sentence in this
;   section. tools/a64/a64_interp.py executes the real compiled image
;   against one flat byte array. It has no data cache, no dirty line, no
;   eviction, no speculation and no second bus master. In that model the
;   CPU's stores and the engine's stores land in the same array in
;   program order, so hazards A, B, C and D DO NOT EXIST. A gate built
;   on it will pass with every DmaCacheRange call in this file deleted.
;
;   That is the same shape of defect as tools/a64/a64_sdio_check.py
;   passing a payload that faults on silicon because its memory was a
;   dictionary that did not care about alignment. A model that is more
;   forgiving than the part certifies bugs.
;
;   COHERENCY IS PROVED ON SILICON OR IT IS NOT PROVED. The instrument
;   is pi4DmaGate.pi4's group G, which runs with the MMU on and carries
;   NEGATIVE CONTROLS: cases that omit the maintenance and must come out
;   WRONG. A coherency test that only ever asserts "it worked" cannot
;   tell a correct discipline from a machine where the caches happened
;   not to matter that afternoon.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE SURFACE, AND EXACTLY WHAT A FUTURE font.pi4 WOULD CALL
; ======================================================================
; RP2350/Lib/font.pico2 is the text engine and it is already portable -
; its own header is unusually honest that "the code is chip-independent"
; and "the file can be used on this chip" are different claims. Every
; executable line of it is glyph arithmetic and PeekB; what stops it
; being portable is the seven names it calls on a display DRIVER.
;
; THIS FILE DOES NOT REWRITE THAT ENGINE. It provides the surface those
; seven names need, so a font.pi4 is font.pico2 with its call sites
; substituted and nothing else touched. The mapping, name for name:
;
;   font.pico2 calls              font.pi4 would call
;   ------------------------      -------------------------------------
;   Tft796Width()                 DisplayWidth()
;   Tft796Height()                DisplayHeight()
;   Tft796Rect(x,y,w,h,c)         DisplayFillRect(x,y,w,h,c)
;                                   - same meaning: a FILLED rectangle.
;                                     This is the call FontTextField()
;                                     uses to stop old digits ghosting.
;   Tft796Window(x0,y0,x1,y1)     nothing - deleted.
;   + SpiWrite8 per pixel           A framebuffer has no address window
;   + SpiFlush                      and no byte stream. The two shapes
;                                   font.pico2 uses a window for are
;                                   both already primitives here:
;                                     a whole cell   -> the caller walks
;                                        rows and calls DisplayHLine()
;                                        per run, or DisplayPixel()
;                                     a run of ink   -> DisplayHLine()
;   Tft796Push(colour, count)     DisplayHLine(x, y, count, colour)
;                                   - FontCharClear() opens a
;                                     single-row window and pushes a
;                                     run; that IS a horizontal line.
;   Tft796PinSet(pin, level)      nothing - deleted. No CS, no DC.
;   tft796_cs, tft796_dc          nothing - deleted.
;
; So the port is: delete the four window/pin/flush calls, replace
; Tft796Push with DisplayHLine, replace Tft796Rect with DisplayFillRect,
; replace the two size accessors. FontUse, FontRange, FontGap,
; FontCharWidth, FontTextWidth and FontHeight need no change at all -
; font.pico2's header already notes those six never touch the panel.
;
; ONE THING A FRAMEBUFFER CAN DO THAT THE ST7796 CANNOT, and it is worth
; knowing before the port: this surface can be READ BACK.
; DisplayGetPixel() and DisplayCopyRect() exist because the pixels are
; in DRAM. font.pico2 has a comment that measuring text "by drawing it
; and looking is not an option on a display that cannot be read back" -
; that constraint is gone here, and the console's scroll is built on its
; absence.
;
; THE GLYPH FORMAT IS font.pico2's, deliberately: 1 bit per pixel, MSB
; of the first byte is the LEFTMOST pixel, rows padded to a whole number
; of bytes so row r of a glyph starts at offset + r*((width+7)/8). That
; is the format tools/make_font.py already emits and the format
; font.pico2 already reads, so a proportional font generated by that
; tool drops straight in.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE BUILT-IN FONT, AND HOW TO REPLACE IT
; ======================================================================
; 8 wide by 16 tall, ASCII 32..126, 95 glyphs, 1520 bytes, at the bottom
; of this file in a DataSection labelled dsp_font8x16. Fixed width, so
; there is no width table and no offset table: glyph n starts at
; n*16 bytes. DejaVu Sans Mono at 13 px, rasterised into an 8x16 cell.
;
; 8x16 rather than 8x8 because the target is HDMI: 1920x1080 at 8x16
; gives 240 columns by 67 rows, which is a usable console, and 8x8 at
; that resolution is unreadable from a desk. DisplayScale() magnifies by
; a whole number for when it needs to be bigger.
;
; IT IS TAKEN BY ADDRESS, NOT BY NAME. DisplayFontUse() stores a pointer,
; so replacing the font is one call and does not touch the renderer:
;
;     DisplayFontUse(?my_glyphs, 8, 16, 32, 126)
;
; and DisplayFontDefault() puts the built-in one back. A proportional
; font needs the width and offset tables and therefore needs the real
; font engine, which is font.pi4's job, not this file's.
;
; Read/Restore is NOT used to reach the data, and that is not a style
; choice. "Read strides 2 bytes whatever the element width - Data.a
; returns every OTHER byte" is an open bug on every target, filed in the
; vault at Bugs/Open bugs.md line 309, open since 2026-08-08. Taking the
; label's address with ? and indexing it with PeekB is both the correct
; tool for a table indexed by character code and the one that is not
; standing on that bug. font.pico2 reaches its tables the same way and
; its generator's header says why.
;
; THE DATA BLOCK IS AT THE END OF THE FILE, but NOT for the reason
; font.pico2 puts its data last, and that difference matters because the
; reason is what makes a rule worth following. On the RP2040 and RP2350
; a kilobyte of DataSection between two pieces of code puts them out of
; reach of each other - Thumb-1's unconditional branch spans +-2 KB. An
; A64 "b" spans +-128 MB. The constraint does not exist on this part, so
; the placement here is legibility only, and a future file that needs
; its data in the middle is free to put it there. Copying the RP2040
; rule across without its reason would have been the stale-recipe
; failure this project has a rule about.
;
; ======================================================================
; PIXEL FORMAT, AND THE ONE THING NO CODE HERE CAN SETTLE
; ======================================================================
; 32 and 16 bits per pixel are supported. Anything else is refused with
; #DSP_EDEPTH rather than half-implemented:
;   24bpp  has no aligned store - every pixel is three bytes straddling
;          word boundaries, and it buys nothing over 32.
;   8bpp   needs a palette, which needs another property tag, which is
;          another number this file cannot cite.
;
; WHICH CHANNEL IS WHICH CANNOT BE DETERMINED WITHOUT LOOKING AT A
; MONITOR. The firmware's default pixel order on a Pi 4 puts blue in the
; low byte, so DisplayRGB() packs (r<<16)|(g<<8)|b for 32bpp - the
; familiar $RRGGBB - and the corresponding 5:6:5 for 16bpp. If red and
; blue come out swapped on the actual screen, that is not a bug to hunt:
;
;     DisplayColourSwap(1)
;
; flips the packing and everything drawn afterwards is right. It is one
; call because it is one bit of information that only an eye can supply.
;
; THE SET-PIXEL-ORDER TAG IS NOW SENT, AND IT WAS NOT FOR A DAY. The
; tag number was unknown until 2026-08-25 and the meaning of its VALUE
; until 2026-08-26; both are cited now
; (Datasheets/pi4/uboot/v2025.01_mbox.h:371 for the tag, :373-374 for
; BGR 0 / RGB 1) and the tag is part of the combined message, which is
; the other half of why - a tag left out of that message is a gap in the
; state the firmware allocates against, not a default left alone.
;
; WHAT IS SENT IS BGR, the arrangement DisplayRGB()'s packing already
; assumes, so the two agree by construction instead of by luck. That
; does NOT make DisplayColourSwap() obsolete: what the firmware calls
; BGR and what comes out of the HDMI socket are still two claims, and
; only an eye joins them. The swap remains the fix, and it is now a fix
; for a disagreement with a KNOWN request rather than with an inherited
; unknown.
;
; The top byte at 32bpp is set to $FF by DisplayRGB(). If the mode is
; XRGB it is ignored; if it is ARGB it means opaque. Setting it is
; correct under both and leaving it clear is wrong under one.
;
; AND SINCE 2026-08-26 THE FIRMWARE IS TOLD TO IGNORE IT. The combined
; message carries set-alpha-mode with #MBX_FB_ALPHA_IGNORED, which is
; value 2 of three at Datasheets/pi4/uboot/v2025.01_mbox.h:393-395. The
; other two are "alpha 0 means opaque" and "alpha 0 means transparent",
; and the first of those would read every $FF this file writes as the
; far end of the scale from opaque. Ignored is the one mode that cannot
; contradict the paragraph above. See DisplayInit() at the line that
; sends it.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT HAS BEEN TESTED, AND WHAT HAS NOT - UPDATED 2026-08-26
; ======================================================================
; THE FIRST THING TO READ, 2026-08-26. THIS LIBRARY HAS RUN ON A PI 4
; AND PRODUCED A 2 x 2 FRAMEBUFFER. That is the most important sentence
; in this section and it postdates everything below it. The seven-
; message bring-up returned #DSP_OK over a surface two pixels square in
; front of a 1920 x 1080 panel, on the boot path where the firmware
; starts the image directly. The gate described further down passed the
; whole time, because the modelled VideoCore answered separate messages
; as though they accumulated - which the real one does not. The gate
; now refuses to, and the library sends one message. See ONE TAG PER
; MESSAGE WAS WRONG at the top of this file.
;
; WHAT IS STILL UNPROVEN AFTER THAT FIX: everything about the real
; firmware. The combined message has been executed against a model that
; was rewritten to behave the way the real firmware is now understood to
; behave, which is a much better test than the one before it and is
; still not a Pi 4. Nothing here has been run on the board since the
; rewrite.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE FIRST THING TO READ AS OF 2026-08-25, kept in place below because
; it is where the arithmetic evidence lives:
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; On 2026-08-25 this library was CALLED for the
; first time, end to end, by
; RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/pi4DisplayProbe.pi4 under
; tools/a64/a64_display_check.py - the compiled artefact executing on the
; project's A64 oracle against a modelled PL011 and a modelled VideoCore.
; DisplayInit() RETURNED #DSP_OK ON THE FIRST ATTEMPT. Every one of the
; three blockers listed below is now cleared, and the run is described
; at the bottom of this section.
;
; STILL TRUE: no pixel from this file has reached a screen, and no image
; containing it has run on a Pi 4. What follows the numbered list is
; evidence about arithmetic, not about glass.
;
; THE THREE BLOCKERS, AS THEY STOOD ON 2026-08-24, kept because a
; blocker that vanishes without trace gets re-derived:
;
;   1. NO MONITOR IS CONNECTED TO THE PI'S HDMI. STILL TRUE. Nothing
;      here can be visually confirmed today by anyone. Every statement
;      about what appears on glass - the shear, the channel order, the
;      legibility of the font at a given resolution - is reasoning.
;   2. mailbox.pi4 DOES NOT EXIST YET. CLEARED - it exists, this file
;      was rewritten onto it, and the seven-message sequence has now
;      been driven through it and watched on the wire.
;   3. THE TAG NUMBERS ARE NOT KNOWN. CLEARED 2026-08-25; see the
;      UPDATE in THE TAG NUMBERS ARE NOT CITED above.
;
; WHAT WAS ACTUALLY DONE, and it is more than "it compiles":
;
;   * Builds clean for -t pi4 behind a test main that stubs the seven
;     mailbox.pi4 entry points. There is no stub in this file, and no
;     copy of mailbox.pi4's numbers in it either.
;   * THE DRAWING HALF WAS RUN, against the project's own A64 oracle
;     (tools/a64/a64_interp.py) with a PL011 modelled on top of it. 69
;     assertions, all passing, over a surface whose pitch is
;     deliberately NOT width*bpp:
;       - a pixel written at row 1 lands at base + 1*pitch, and the byte
;         at base + width*bpp is still zero. That pair is the shear bug,
;         asserted rather than reasoned about.
;       - every pixel of a rendered glyph cell compared against the bit
;         the font table actually holds, opaque and transparent - which
;         checks the MSB-first bit order, the row stride, the glyph
;         stride and the run loop together.
;       - clipping on all four edges, including that a fully off-surface
;         write leaves all 9600 bytes untouched.
;       - the console: wrap, scroll, CR, LF, backspace-and-erase, tab,
;         cursor clamp.
;       - both depths, both copy directions, and the refusal of the one
;         overlapping copy the loop cannot do.
;   * A 240x96 console screen was rendered into a modelled framebuffer
;     and the memory dumped back out as an image. It reads correctly and
;     it does not lean, at a pitch of 992 against a width of 240.
;     THAT IS A PICTURE OF MEMORY, NOT OF A MONITOR - it proves the row
;     arithmetic and the glyph data, and it proves nothing whatever
;     about HDMI, the firmware, or the channel order.
;   * The emitted assembly was read for the four things meant to be true
;     of it: every row address is base + y*pitch (58 multiplies in the
;     image, 12 fed by dsp_pitch, ZERO fed by dsp_w), the 32-bit
;     accesses are str w / strh w and not str x, the 16-byte alignment
;     is a shift pair and not a mask, and dc cvac assembled to
;     $D50B7A29.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; 2026-08-25 - THE FIRST END-TO-END RUN. tools/a64/a64_display_check.py.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; The probe was built exactly as the board will get it - --load-addr
; 0x400000 --stack-addr 0x3000000 --entry-returns - and the resulting
; image executed instruction by instruction. The modelled firmware
; CLAMPS 1920x1080 to 160x96 and returns a pitch of 704 where width*bpp
; is 640, because a model that agreed with the request would have tested
; nothing. DisplayInit() returned #DSP_OK, and:
;
;   * 39 assertions inside the probe, all passing, over the clamped
;     geometry: the bus-to-ARM conversion against the alias selector,
;     the pitch, the padding, the four rectangles, the one-pixel frame,
;     clipping at x = width, and the console.
;   * 17 more in the harness, computed there and never read back out of
;     the program, so they cannot inherit its arithmetic. The strongest
;     is one the program cannot make about itself: ALL 6144 PADDING
;     BYTES ARE STILL ZERO. Rows addressed as y*width*bpp land in the
;     previous row's padding; rows addressed as y*pitch cannot reach it.
;   * The drawn 'H' compared pixel by pixel against the font bytes read
;     out of the IMAGE FILE - 128 of 128 - which checks the MSB-first
;     bit order, the glyph stride, the row stride and DisplayChar()'s
;     run loop together rather than one at a time.
;   * THE GATE WAS WATCHED FAILING, which is the only way to know a gate
;     works. `--mutate` rebuilds this file with every "* dsp_pitch)"
;     replaced by "* (dsp_w * dsp_bpp))" - the exact bug the PITCH
;     section of this header warns about - and 8 harness assertions and
;     16 of the probe's 39 go red. The mutant lives in scratchpad and
;     nothing under RaspberryPi4/ is touched.
;   * ONE DEFECT WAS FOUND AND FIXED. DisplayStandardTags() was filling
;     the set-pixel-order slot, which made DisplayInit() command the
;     firmware's channel layout to an uncited zero. It was visible only
;     because the harness prints the tag sequence the firmware received.
;     See the note on DisplayStandardTags().
;   * The cache maintenance was MEASURED rather than assumed: a
;     full-surface DisplayFlush() on its own cost 2513 counter ticks
;     against 1056 cache lines, and auto-flush made a fill 33805 ticks
;     where the same fill without it was 31292. The dc cvac loop
;     executes. Whether cvac is the RIGHT operation for this reader is
;     still a question only a monitor can answer.
;
; WHAT THAT RUN DOES NOT SHOW, and the list is short and important: the
; real firmware's answers, whether it allocates anything at all with
; HDMI unplugged, the real pitch for a real mode, the channel order, and
; whether cleaned pixels actually reach the display controller.
;
; TWO TOOLCHAIN FINDINGS FROM THE SAME AFTERNOON, NEITHER OF THEM THIS
; FILE'S DOING, recorded because a build failure that is not yours is
; information:
;
;   * pmfc.exe WAS REPLACED MID-SESSION with the 64-bit build. Earlier
;     the same day the shipped pmfc.exe was still the 32-bit model and a
;     separate pmfc_64.exe carried the widening; the canary for telling
;     them apart is a literal wider than 32 bits, which the 32-bit model
;     rejects with "does not fit ... (.i is the native integer and is 4
;     bytes on THIS target)". If that warning appears, the compiler does
;     not implement the model this file is written for.
;   * tools/a64_run.exe CANNOT RUN ANY IMAGE THE CURRENT COMPILER EMITS.
;     It stops at step 7 with "unsupported A64 word $90000409", which is
;     adrp, in the BSS-zero loop of the standard prologue. This is not
;     about this file: a freshly built, unmodified
;     Examples/01_GettingStarted/pi4Hello.pi4 fails identically, while
;     the pi4Hello.img already committed - built before the emitter
;     moved from adr to adrp - still runs. The emulator is behind the
;     backend. Its --selftest passes, so the gap is the decoder, not the
;     build. Until it is taught adrp, the Python oracle at
;     tools/a64/a64_interp.py is the one that can run pi4 images; it
;     decodes adrp and already carries dc cvac in its maintenance table.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE WHOLE API, IN ONE PLACE
; ======================================================================
;   configuration, before anything else
;     DisplaySetTag(slot, value)      fill one framebuffer tag number
;     DisplayTagSet(slot)             1 if that slot has been filled
;     DisplayTagsReady()              1 when all five required are in
;     DisplayMissingSlot()            the first required slot still unset
;     DisplayPixelOrder(v)            value for the optional order tag
;
;   bring-up
;     DisplayInit(w, h, depth)        #DSP_OK, or a negative #DSP_E...
;     DisplayLastError()              the code from the last DisplayInit
;     DisplayReady()                  1 once there is a surface
;
;   what we got
;     DisplayWidth()  DisplayHeight()  DisplayPitch()  DisplayDepth()
;     DisplayBytesPerPixel()  DisplayBase()  DisplayBusBase()
;     DisplaySize()
;     DisplayPanelWidth()  DisplayPanelHeight()
;                                     what the panel said it ALREADY was,
;                                     before anything was set, or 0
;
;   colour
;     DisplayRGB(r, g, b)             r,g,b are 0..255
;     DisplayColourSwap(0|1)          if red and blue are the wrong way
;
;   surface
;     DisplayClear(colour)
;     DisplayPixel(x, y, colour)      clipped
;     DisplayGetPixel(x, y)           -1 outside the surface
;     DisplayHLine(x, y, w, colour)   the font engine's run primitive
;     DisplayVLine(x, y, h, colour)
;     DisplayFillRect(x, y, w, h, colour)
;     DisplayFrameRect(x, y, w, h, colour)     one-pixel outline
;     DisplayBlit(*src, srcPitch, x, y, w, h)  native-format pixels in
;     DisplayCopyRect(sx, sy, dx, dy, w, h)    surface to surface
;
;   coherency
;     DisplayAutoFlush(0|1)  DisplayFlush()  DisplayFlushRect(x,y,w,h)
;
;   text
;     DisplayFontUse(addr, cellW, cellH, first, last)
;     DisplayFontDefault()  DisplayScale(n)
;     DisplayCharWidth()  DisplayCharHeight()
;     DisplayChar(x, y, ch, fg, bg)       opaque, returns the advance
;     DisplayCharClear(x, y, ch, fg)      transparent
;     DisplayText(x, y, *s, fg, bg)       returns the width drawn
;     DisplayTextClear(x, y, *s, fg)
;     DisplayTextWidth(*s)
;
;   console
;     DisplayConsole(x, y, w, h)      the window, in pixels
;     DisplayConsoleFull()            the whole surface
;     DisplayColour(fg, bg)  DisplayCls()
;     DisplayLocate(col, row)  DisplayCols()  DisplayRows()
;     DisplayCursorCol()  DisplayCursorRow()
;     DisplayPutC(ch)                 handles 13 10 8 9
;     DisplayPrint(*s)  DisplayPrintLn(*s)  DisplayNewLine()
;     DisplayPrintDec(v)  DisplayPrintHex(v, digits)
;     DisplayScroll(rows)
;
; This file does NOT turn EnableExplicit on. The pi4 tree does not use
; it (anvil.pi4 and pi4Hello.pi4 both predate the habit), and a
; library that changes the language rules for everything compiled after
; it is a caller-visible side effect that should not arrive by accident
; from a display driver.
; ======================================================================


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Slot numbers for the firmware tags this file will not hardcode.
; See THE TAG NUMBERS ARE NOT CITED in the header.
;
; SLOTS 0..6 KEEP THE NUMBERS THEY HAVE ALWAYS HAD. Three arrived on
; 2026-08-26 with the combined message and they were appended rather
; than inserted in protocol order, because a caller that wrote
; DisplaySetTag(5, ...) against the old table would otherwise be
; configuring a different tag with no warning at all. Read
; DisplayInit() for the order the tags actually go out in; this list is
; a table of slots, not a sequence.
;
; EVERY SLOT IS REQUIRED NOW, and slots 5 and 6 used to be optional.
; That changed with the combined message and it is not tidying: the
; firmware allocates against the state ONE transaction accumulated, so
; a tag left out of that transaction is not "the firmware's default left
; alone" any more - it is a hole in the state the allocate reads. U-Boot
; sends all nine every time (v2025.01_msg.c:166-189) and so does this.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#DSP_TAG_SETPHYS     = 0
#DSP_TAG_SETVIRT     = 1
#DSP_TAG_SETDEPTH    = 2
#DSP_TAG_ALLOCATE    = 3
#DSP_TAG_GETPITCH    = 4
#DSP_TAG_SETORDER    = 5
#DSP_TAG_SETOFFSET   = 6
#DSP_TAG_SETALPHA    = 7       ; added 2026-08-26
#DSP_TAG_SETOVERSCAN = 8       ; added 2026-08-26
#DSP_TAG_GETPHYS     = 9       ; added 2026-08-26
; ---- the display-selection three, added 2026-08-26 -------------------
; THESE THREE ARE NOT REQUIRED AND THAT IS THE POINT. #DSP_SLOT_REQUIRED
; stays at ten, so DisplayTagsReady(), DisplayMissingSlot() and
; DisplayInit()'s refusal all behave exactly as they did: a program that
; never mentions a display still gets the same framebuffer, through the
; same two messages, carrying the same nine tags. Everything about
; selection is opt-in, and the opt-out path is not merely "still
; supported" - it is byte for byte the traffic that was on the wire
; before any of this was written.
;
; They are slots rather than bare constants for the same reason the
; other ten are: a firmware that numbers one of them differently can be
; accommodated from the caller without editing this file.
; DisplayStandardTags() fills them along with the rest.
#DSP_TAG_SETDISPLAYNUM  = 10
#DSP_TAG_GETNUMDISPLAYS = 11
#DSP_TAG_GETDISPLAYID   = 12
#DSP_SLOT_COUNT      = 13
#DSP_SLOT_REQUIRED   = 10      ; the first ten, and only those. The count
                               ; and the requirement used to be the same
                               ; number and are deliberately not any more.

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Return codes. DisplayInit() returns one of these and stashes it for
; DisplayLastError(). Positive is success; every failure is negative and
; distinct, so a caller printing the number is told what went wrong
; without a serial trace.
;
; THERE IS STILL A CODE PER TAG, and keeping that through the move to a
; single combined message took a little work rather than none. When nine
; tags travel together the transport reports one failure for the whole
; buffer - but it reports WHICH TAG the firmware left unanswered
; (MailboxListFailedTag()), and DisplayInit() maps that index back onto
; the per-tag code below. So a wrong tag number still surfaces as the
; code for exactly the tag whose number was wrong, which is what the
; caller needs and what the old seven-message shape gave for free.
;
; Call MailboxError() straight afterwards for the transport's own reason
; (#MBX_ERR_TAG says "that tag id means nothing to this firmware").
;
; -1..-15 ARE UNCHANGED. Nothing that printed those numbers, and the
; probe prints all of them, has to be re-read.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#DSP_OK         =  1
#DSP_ENOTAGS    = -1           ; a required tag slot is still unset
#DSP_EARGS      = -2           ; width or height not positive
#DSP_EDEPTH     = -3           ; depth is not 16 or 32
#DSP_ENOMAILBOX = -4           ; MailboxInit() failed - see MailboxError()
#DSP_ESETPHYS   = -5           ; the set-physical-size tag failed
#DSP_ESETVIRT   = -6           ; the set-virtual-size tag failed
#DSP_ESETDEPTH  = -7           ; the set-depth tag failed
#DSP_ESETORDER  = -8           ; the set-pixel-order tag failed
#DSP_ESETOFFSET = -9           ; the set-virtual-offset tag failed
#DSP_EALLOC     = -10          ; the allocate-buffer tag failed
#DSP_EGETPITCH  = -11          ; the get-pitch tag failed
#DSP_ENOBUFFER  = -12          ; allocate returned a zero base
#DSP_EPITCH     = -13          ; pitch zero, or below width*bpp
#DSP_ESIZE      = -14          ; allocated size below pitch*height
#DSP_EMODE      = -15          ; firmware clamped to an unusable geometry
; ---- added 2026-08-26, with the combined message ---------------------
#DSP_ESETALPHA    = -16        ; the set-alpha-mode tag failed
#DSP_ESETOVERSCAN = -17        ; the set-overscan tag failed
#DSP_ECOMPOSE     = -18        ; the nine-tag buffer would not compose -
                               ; #MBX_ERR_TOOBIG from the transport, which
                               ; means #MBX_MAX_BYTES in mailbox.pi4 is
                               ; smaller than this file's message
#DSP_ESEND        = -19        ; the message went out and the transaction
                               ; itself failed - timeout, wrong echo, or a
                               ; whole-buffer response code that was not
                               ; SUCCESS. MailboxError() says which.
; ---- added 2026-08-26, with display selection ------------------------
#DSP_ESELECT      = -20        ; the set-display-num tag failed. Only
                               ; reachable when DisplaySelect() has been
                               ; called: the tag is not sent otherwise.
                               ; The likely meaning is that this firmware
                               ; has no such tag, which MailboxError()
                               ; reports as #MBX_ERR_TAG.
#DSP_ESELINDEX    = -21        ; DisplaySelect() was given a negative
                               ; index, or one at or past the number of
                               ; displays the firmware admits to. NOT
                               ; returned by DisplayInit(); it is
                               ; DisplaySelect()'s own refusal, left in
                               ; DisplayLastError() so the caller can see
                               ; which of the two reasons it was.

Global Dim dsp_cfg.i[#DSP_SLOT_COUNT]
Global dsp_cfgset.i = 0        ; bit n set = slot n has been given a value

Global dsp_ready.i  = 0
Global dsp_err.i    = 0
Global dsp_base.i   = 0        ; ARM physical address of pixel (0,0)
Global dsp_bus.i    = 0        ; the raw bus address the firmware returned
Global dsp_size.i   = 0        ; bytes the firmware allocated
Global dsp_w.i      = 0
Global dsp_h.i      = 0
Global dsp_pitch.i  = 0        ; BYTES per row, from the firmware
; DOUBLE BUFFERING / VSYNC. dsp_virtMul asks DisplayInit for a VIRTUAL
; surface this many times the physical height, so a page-flipping caller
; has a second (off-screen) buffer stacked below the first. Default 1 -
; virtual == physical, exactly as before. dsp_viewOff is the byte offset
; within the buffer of the origin currently being scanned out (what
; DisplayFlip set), so a screenshot can read the half that is actually on
; the glass rather than always the top one.
Global dsp_virtMul.i = 1
Global dsp_viewOff.i = 0
Global dsp_depth.i  = 0        ; bits per pixel
Global dsp_bpp.i    = 0        ; BYTES per pixel
Global dsp_swap.i   = 0        ; 1 = red and blue the other way round
                               ; (a SOFTWARE flip, applied by DisplayRGB)
Global dsp_order.i  = #MBX_FB_ORDER_BGR
                               ; the value sent with the set-pixel-order
                               ; tag, which is now sent every time. BGR is
                               ; the firmware's own default on this part
                               ; and the value the bench measured on the
                               ; direct-firmware boot; see
                               ; DisplayPixelOrder().
Global dsp_panelw.i = 0        ; what get-physical said before anything
Global dsp_panelh.i = 0        ; was set. 0 = it would not say.

; ---- display selection, added 2026-08-26 -----------------------------
; -1 IS "NOBODY CHOSE" AND IT IS NOT THE SAME AS 0. Index 0 is a real
; display and on a board with the cable in HDMI1 it IS HDMI1. The whole
; failure this feature exists to end was a program treating "the one we
; got" as "the first one"; a sentinel that could be mistaken for an
; index would reintroduce it inside this file.
Global dsp_sel.i    = -1       ; the display INDEX DisplaySelect() chose,
                               ; sent as the value of set-display-num at
                               ; the head of every framebuffer message.
Global dsp_actid.i  = -1       ; cached answer of DisplayActiveId(), the
                               ; DispmanX ID we believe we are on. -1 =
                               ; not established, or not yet asked.
Global dsp_ndisp.i  = -1       ; cached answer of DisplayCount(). -1 =
                               ; not yet asked, or the firmware refused.

Global dsp_auto.i   = 1        ; auto-flush the D-cache after each draw
Global dsp_flushaddr.i = 0     ; the one line DisplayCacheLine() cleans

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE DMA SEAM'S STATE. See THE DMA SEAM in the header.
;
;  THESE ARE DECLARED UNCONDITIONALLY, OUTSIDE ANY CompilerIf, AND THAT
;  IS DELIBERATE. Six cells of BSS is the entire cost at
;  #DSP_DMA_LINKED = 0, and in exchange the ACCESSORS can also be
;  unconditional - so a program can call DisplayUseDma(1) or read
;  DisplayDmaOps() whether or not the blitter was linked, and get an
;  honest zero rather than a compile error. A diagnostic that has to be
;  wrapped in the same CompilerIf as the thing it reports on is a
;  diagnostic that cannot report "it was not linked", which is the one
;  answer it most needs to be able to give.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Global dsp_dmaOn.i    = 0      ; the RUN switch. Off even when linked.
Global dsp_dmaRead.i  = 1      ; post-transfer invalidate. See hazard B.
Global dsp_dmaMin.i   = 4096   ; pixels below which the CPU is used anyway
Global dsp_dmaOps.i   = 0      ; transfers the engine actually performed
Global dsp_dmaBack.i  = 0      ; times the CPU path was taken instead
Global dsp_dmaFail.i  = 0      ; times the engine was asked and refused

Global dsp_fbits.i  = 0        ; address of the glyph bitmap blob
Global dsp_fw.i     = 0        ; cell width in pixels
Global dsp_fh.i     = 0        ; cell height in rows
Global dsp_frb.i    = 0        ; bytes per glyph row = (cellW + 7) / 8
Global dsp_ffirst.i = 32
Global dsp_flast.i  = 126
Global dsp_scale.i  = 1

Global dsp_cox.i    = 0        ; console origin, in pixels
Global dsp_coy.i    = 0
Global dsp_cw.i     = 0        ; console size, in pixels
Global dsp_ch.i     = 0
Global dsp_cols.i   = 0
Global dsp_rows.i   = 0
Global dsp_cx.i     = 0        ; cursor, in cells
Global dsp_cy.i     = 0
Global dsp_fg.i     = 0
Global dsp_bg.i     = 0
Global dsp_schunk.i = 8        ; rows scrolled at once when the console
                               ; runs off the bottom. See the long note
                               ; on DisplayScrollChunk() for why the
                               ; default is not 1 and what it costs the
                               ; reader. Clamped in DisplayNewLine(),
                               ; not here, because dsp_rows moves.


; ======================================================================
;  CONFIGURATION - the firmware constants this file will not guess
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplaySetTag - give one slot its value. Returns 1, or 0 if the slot
; number is out of range.
;
; The value is masked to 32 bits because that is what goes into the
; property buffer, and because a caller who writes a negative number by
; accident should not be able to smuggle the top half of a 64-bit .i
; into a protocol word.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DisplaySetTag(slot.i, value.i)
  If slot < 0 Or slot >= #DSP_SLOT_COUNT
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  dsp_cfg[slot] = value & $FFFFFFFF
  ; PARENTHESISED: | binds tighter than <<, so "dsp_cfgset | 1 << slot"
  ; would be "(dsp_cfgset | 1) << slot".
  dsp_cfgset = dsp_cfgset | (1 << slot)
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; DisplayStandardTags - fill the slots from mailbox.pi4's cited
; framebuffer tag constants.
;
; ADDED 2026-08-25, when the numbers stopped being uncitable. The header
; above says this file's slots stay slots and the values live in
; mailbox.pi4 - they do. This is the one line that connects them, so a
; caller who wants the standard Raspberry Pi framebuffer does not have to
; copy ten constants by hand and get one wrong. (It was seven when this
; was written; the combined message added three more, which is three
; more chances to mistype one.)
;
; It is NOT called from DisplayInit(). The refusal stays the default:
; a caller that never asks for the standard tags still gets #DSP_ENOTAGS
; rather than a silent set of numbers it did not choose. Filling the
; slots remains a deliberate act, which is the whole point of the slot
; mechanism - it is just no longer a research project.
;
;     DisplayStandardTags()
;     DisplayInit(1920, 1080, 32)
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; IT NOW FILLS THE SET-PIXEL-ORDER SLOT, AND IT SPENT A DAY REFUSING TO.
; The reversal is written out rather than the old paragraph deleted,
; because the reasoning behind the refusal was sound and exactly one of
; its premises stopped being true.
;
; WHAT THE REFUSAL SAID, 2026-08-25. The first draft filled all seven
; slots. Filling slot 5 made DisplayInit() send the set-pixel-order tag
; carrying dsp_order, which was ZERO unless DisplayPixelOrder() had been
; called - so one convenience call was quietly commanding the firmware's
; channel layout to a number nobody had chosen. The tag NUMBER had
; become citable that day; the meaning of its VALUE had not. Which state
; word means RGB and which means BGR was not in
; Datasheets/pi4/raspberrypi-firmware.h - that file carries the enum and
; nothing else - and it was nowhere else on this disk. (The
; HVS_PIXEL_ORDER_* constants in the vc4 register header are the
; hardware scaler's own field and a DIFFERENT device from this property
; tag. Citing them would have been the same conflation the mailbox
; already cost this project once, when chapter 13 of the BCM2711 manual
; was cited for the wrong mailbox.) And it aimed straight at the one
; failure this file predicts it cannot fix by reasoning - red and blue
; the wrong way round, with the cause hidden one layer below where
; anybody would look for it.
;
; HOW IT WAS FOUND, because "it was reasoned about" and "it was observed"
; are different claims: tools/a64/a64_display_check.py executes the
; probe against a modelled VideoCore and PRINTS THE TAG SEQUENCE the
; firmware actually received. It read
;   SETPHYS SETVIRT SETDEPTH SETORDER SETOFFSET ALLOCATE GETPITCH
; and SETORDER had no business being in it.
;
; WHAT CHANGED, 2026-08-26. Two things, and either alone would have left
; the refusal standing.
;
;   1. THE VALUE IS CITED NOW. U-Boot's own mailbox header arrived on
;      this disk with the framebuffer investigation:
;      Datasheets/pi4/uboot/v2025.01_mbox.h:373-374 defines
;      BCM2835_MBOX_PIXEL_ORDER_BGR 0 and BCM2835_MBOX_PIXEL_ORDER_RGB 1,
;      immediately below the tag number at :371. mailbox.pi4 carries
;      both as #MBX_FB_ORDER_BGR and #MBX_FB_ORDER_RGB. The premise the
;      refusal rested on is simply gone.
;   2. LEAVING A TAG OUT IS NO LONGER "LEAVING THE DEFAULT ALONE". The
;      nine tags travel in ONE message now and the firmware allocates
;      against the state that one message accumulated. A tag omitted
;      from the transaction is a hole in what the allocate reads, not a
;      polite abstention. U-Boot sends it every time
;      (v2025.01_msg.c:175-176) and U-Boot is the code that
;      demonstrably brings this board's HDMI up.
;
; THE VALUE SENT IS #MBX_FB_ORDER_BGR, and that is measured rather than
; picked. RaspberryPi4/Monitor/anvil.pi4's ScreenUp() records the
; observation: booted straight from the firmware, get-pixel-order
; answers 0, and this file's packing - (r<<16)|(g<<8)|b - is correct on
; that path with no swap at all. Booted through U-Boot the same tag
; answered 1 and the swap was needed. Asking for BGR asks for the
; arrangement this file's packing already assumes, on BOTH boot paths,
; instead of inheriting whichever one the loader happened to leave.
;
; TO COMMAND SOMETHING ELSE DELIBERATELY, one call, and it says what it
; means now that the constant has a name:
;
;     DisplayPixelOrder(#MBX_FB_ORDER_RGB)
;
; And DisplayColourSwap(1) is still the answer when an eye says the
; channels are the wrong way round, because an eye outranks every
; paragraph above it.
;
; THE OTHER TWO NEW SLOTS are in the message for the same reason and
; both carry values whose meaning is cited: set-alpha-mode with
; #MBX_FB_ALPHA_IGNORED (v2025.01_mbox.h:395) and set-overscan with zero
; on all four edges (v2025.01_msg.c:182-186). DisplayInit() explains why
; IGNORED and not one of the other two, at the line that sends it.
;
; #DSP_TAG_SETOFFSET WAS ALWAYS FILLED and the reason it survived the
; refusal is the reason the other two are safe to add: is the VALUE that
; will be sent one whose meaning can be cited? DisplayInit() sends the
; offset as a hardcoded (0, 0), which means "no pan" under any reading,
; and it defends against a virtual offset left behind by a previous
; mode. Zero was not a guess. Zero for the pixel order would have been.
;
; #DSP_TAG_GETPHYS is the tenth slot and the odd one out: it is the tag
; for the message sent BEFORE the combined one, asking the panel what it
; already is. It is a slot like the rest so that a caller talking to a
; firmware that numbers it differently can redirect it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; Returns 1 always; there is nothing here that can fail.
Procedure.i DisplayStandardTags()
  DisplaySetTag(#DSP_TAG_SETPHYS,     #MBX_TAG_FB_SETPHYS)
  DisplaySetTag(#DSP_TAG_SETVIRT,     #MBX_TAG_FB_SETVIRT)
  DisplaySetTag(#DSP_TAG_SETDEPTH,    #MBX_TAG_FB_SETDEPTH)
  DisplaySetTag(#DSP_TAG_ALLOCATE,    #MBX_TAG_FB_ALLOCATE)
  DisplaySetTag(#DSP_TAG_GETPITCH,    #MBX_TAG_FB_GETPITCH)
  DisplaySetTag(#DSP_TAG_SETORDER,    #MBX_TAG_FB_SETORDER)
  DisplaySetTag(#DSP_TAG_SETOFFSET,   #MBX_TAG_FB_SETOFFSET)
  DisplaySetTag(#DSP_TAG_SETALPHA,    #MBX_TAG_FB_SETALPHA)
  DisplaySetTag(#DSP_TAG_SETOVERSCAN, #MBX_TAG_FB_SETOVERSCAN)
  DisplaySetTag(#DSP_TAG_GETPHYS,     #MBX_TAG_FB_GETPHYS)
  ; The selection three. Filled here so a caller never has to know they
  ; are separate; NOT required, and not sent unless DisplaySelect() has
  ; been called or DisplayCount()/DisplayIdAt() are asked directly.
  DisplaySetTag(#DSP_TAG_SETDISPLAYNUM,  #MBX_TAG_FB_SETDISPLAYNUM)
  DisplaySetTag(#DSP_TAG_GETNUMDISPLAYS, #MBX_TAG_FB_GETNUMDISPLAYS)
  DisplaySetTag(#DSP_TAG_GETDISPLAYID,   #MBX_TAG_FB_GETDISPLAYID)
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; DisplayTagSet - 1 if that slot has been given a value.
Procedure.i DisplayTagSet(slot.i)
  If slot < 0 Or slot >= #DSP_SLOT_COUNT
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (dsp_cfgset & (1 << slot)) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; DisplayMissingSlot - the number of the first REQUIRED slot still
; unset, or -1 when they are all in. Exists so a bring-up program can
; print which one is missing instead of just "it refused".
Procedure.i DisplayMissingSlot()
  Define i.i
  i = 0
  While i < #DSP_SLOT_REQUIRED
    If (dsp_cfgset & (1 << i)) = 0
      ProcedureReturn i
    EndIf
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DisplayTagsReady()
  If DisplayMissingSlot() < 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure



; ======================================================================
;  BRING-UP
;
;  There is NO property buffer in this file. mailbox.pi4 owns the buffer,
;  its alignment, its cache maintenance and its bus address; this file
;  supplies tag numbers and words and reads the answers back. What
;  changed on 2026-08-26 is not that ownership - it is that mailbox.pi4
;  can now be asked to carry SEVERAL tags in one message, and this file
;  asks it to. See THE MAILBOX CONTRACT in the header for the whole
;  story, including the seven-message version that produced a 2 x 2
;  screen and the reasoning that made it look correct at the time.
; ======================================================================

; How many tags DisplayInit()'s combined message carries. U-Boot's
; struct msg_setup has exactly this many (v2025.01_msg.c:36-45) and
; this file sends the same nine, in the same order.
#DSP_MSG_TAGS = 9

; Where each slot's tag ended up in that message, or -1 for a slot that
; is not in it. Written by DisplayInit() as it composes, and read back
; only to turn the transport's "tag number N was not answered" into the
; per-tag #DSP_E... code the caller has always been given.
Global Dim dsp_tix.i[#DSP_SLOT_COUNT]

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DspAdd - append one slot's tag to the message being composed.
;
; A thin wrapper, but it is the one place the slot-to-tag-number lookup
; happens, so a reader of DisplayInit() sees the SEQUENCE without the
; lookup spelled out nine times. Returns the tag's index in the list, or
; -1 if it would not fit.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DspAdd(slot.i, reqBytes.i, respBytes.i)
  dsp_tix[slot] = MailboxListAdd(dsp_cfg[slot], reqBytes, respBytes)
  ProcedureReturn dsp_tix[slot]
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DspStep - lay out a ONE-TAG message for the slot given.
;
; Kept, and kept under its old name, because one query genuinely is its
; own message: asking the panel what mode it is in has to happen BEFORE
; the message that tells it what mode to be in, so it cannot be a tag in
; that message. Everything else moved to DspAdd().
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DspStep(slot.i, reqBytes.i, respBytes.i)
  ProcedureReturn MailboxBegin(dsp_cfg[slot], reqBytes, respBytes)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DspSlotError - the #DSP_E... code that belongs to one slot.
;
; A table written as a ladder rather than an array because an array
; would need a parallel list of constants kept in step by hand, and the
; ladder cannot get out of step with the slot numbers it names.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DspSlotError(slot.i)
  If slot = #DSP_TAG_SETPHYS
    ProcedureReturn #DSP_ESETPHYS
  ElseIf slot = #DSP_TAG_SETVIRT
    ProcedureReturn #DSP_ESETVIRT
  ElseIf slot = #DSP_TAG_SETDEPTH
    ProcedureReturn #DSP_ESETDEPTH
  ElseIf slot = #DSP_TAG_SETORDER
    ProcedureReturn #DSP_ESETORDER
  ElseIf slot = #DSP_TAG_SETALPHA
    ProcedureReturn #DSP_ESETALPHA
  ElseIf slot = #DSP_TAG_SETOFFSET
    ProcedureReturn #DSP_ESETOFFSET
  ElseIf slot = #DSP_TAG_SETOVERSCAN
    ProcedureReturn #DSP_ESETOVERSCAN
  ElseIf slot = #DSP_TAG_ALLOCATE
    ProcedureReturn #DSP_EALLOC
  ElseIf slot = #DSP_TAG_GETPITCH
    ProcedureReturn #DSP_EGETPITCH
  ElseIf slot = #DSP_TAG_SETDISPLAYNUM
    ProcedureReturn #DSP_ESELECT
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #DSP_ESEND
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DspFailedCode - which tag did the firmware refuse to answer?
;
; THIS IS WHAT KEEPS "A CODE PER TAG" ALIVE ACROSS THE MOVE TO ONE
; MESSAGE. Nine tags in one buffer means one transport failure for the
; lot of them, and a caller told only "the mailbox failed" would have to
; guess which number was wrong. mailbox.pi4 checks every tag's own
; response bit and remembers the index of the first one the firmware
; left clear; this turns that index back into the slot, and the slot
; into the code the caller has been reading since the file was written.
;
; Falls back to #DSP_ESEND, because a failure that cannot be attributed
; to a tag is a failure of the transaction and should say so rather than
; blame whichever tag happens to be first.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DspFailedCode()
  Define bad.i
  Define s.i
  bad = MailboxListFailedTag()
  If bad >= 0
    s = 0
    While s < #DSP_SLOT_COUNT
      If dsp_tix[s] = bad
        ProcedureReturn DspSlotError(s)
      EndIf
      s = s + 1
    Wend
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #DSP_ESEND
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  DISPLAY SELECTION - WHICH OF THE TWO HDMI SOCKETS
; ======================================================================
;
;  THE HOLE THIS FILLS. A Pi 4 has two HDMI sockets. Until 2026-08-26
;  nothing here ever told the firmware which one to use: no selection
;  tag was sent at all, so whichever display the firmware picked at boot
;  is the one we silently accepted, and there was no way even to REPORT
;  which that had been. The monitor's "no framebuffer" message said
;  "HDMI0 was not connected", which was not a deduction from anything -
;  it was a guess, and for anybody with the cable in HDMI1 it was a
;  guess that sent them to the wrong end of the board.
;
;  ---- THE PROTOCOL, AND WHERE IT WAS READ --------------------------
;  The tag numbers, the two numberings and the driver's exact usage are
;  written up at length in RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4 under THE
;  DISPLAY SELECTION TAGS. It is not repeated here. The three sentences
;  that decide the shape of this file are:
;
;    * GET_NUM_DISPLAYS and GET_DISPLAY_ID are each a message of their
;      own (vc4_firmware_kms.c:1958-1960 and :1990-1994).
;    * GET_DISPLAY_ID is in-and-out through one word: an INDEX goes in,
;      a DispmanX ID comes back. Index 0 does NOT mean HDMI0.
;    * SET_DISPLAY_NUM is never a message of its own in the driver. It
;      is the FIRST TAG of a message whose LATER TAGS it steers
;      (vc4_firmware_kms.c:1845-1850, :1863).
;
;  ---- THE ONE THING NO SOURCE SETTLES ------------------------------
;  WHETHER THE SELECTION STICKS PAST THE END OF THE MESSAGE. The driver
;  sends SET_DISPLAY_NUM once and never depends on it again; every other
;  display-aware tag it sends carries the display number as a field of
;  its own value buffer. The firmware project's property-interface
;  documentation does not mention the tag at all. So there are two
;  possible worlds and this disk cannot tell us which one we are in.
;
;  THE RULE HERE IS THE ONE THAT IS RIGHT IN BOTH:
;
;      EVERY MESSAGE THAT TOUCHES THE FRAMEBUFFER LEADS WITH THE
;      SELECTION, WHENEVER A SELECTION IS IN FORCE.
;
;  Both of DisplayInit()'s messages, not just the big one. If selection
;  is message-scoped, sending it twice is REQUIRED - otherwise the
;  get-physical query would report one socket's mode and the nine-tag
;  message would configure the other's, which is a way to get a
;  perfectly successful init at completely the wrong geometry. If
;  selection is sticky, the second send is the same index again and
;  changes nothing. The one shape that is wrong under either answer is
;  the tempting one - a lone SET_DISPLAY_NUM message, then assume the
;  next message inherits it - and this file does not do it.
;
;  ---- AND WHAT THAT COSTS THE PATH THAT WORKS ----------------------
;  NOTHING, DELIBERATELY, AND IT IS CHECKED. With no selection made,
;  DisplayInit() composes exactly the two messages it composed before -
;  a lone get-physical, then nine tags in U-Boot's order - and no
;  selection tag goes out anywhere. The nine-tag transaction and its
;  ordering were expensive to arrive at (see THE MAILBOX CONTRACT) and
;  none of it is disturbed: the selection tag goes IN FRONT of the nine,
;  never among them, and only when asked for.
;  tools/a64/a64_display_check.py asserts both halves - that the default
;  run sends no selection tag at all, and that the selected run leads
;  both messages with it and nothing else moves.
;
;  ---- WHAT CANNOT BE ANSWERED, AND IS NOT PRETENDED AWAY -----------
;  THERE IS NO TAG THAT ASKS "WHICH DISPLAY IS THE FRAMEBUFFER ON". Not
;  in the downstream header, not in the driver, not in the property
;  documentation. So with no selection made and more than one display
;  attached, DisplayActiveId() answers -1 and means it. The one
;  inference it will make is the sound one: if the firmware found
;  exactly ONE display, there was nothing else it could have chosen.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DspMailbox - bring the transport up if it is not already.
;
; The selection queries can be called before DisplayInit(), so they
; cannot assume what DisplayInit() assumes. Same two lines DisplayInit()
; has always had, in one place because three procedures now need them.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DspMailbox()
  If MailboxReady() <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn MailboxInit()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayCount - how many displays did the firmware find at boot?
;
; Returns the count, or -1 for "the firmware would not say". THOSE TWO
; ARE NOT THE SAME AS ZERO and the difference matters on this board:
; zero is the answer you get when nothing was plugged in at power-on -
; the firmware only probes at boot - and it is a real, actionable
; answer. -1 means the tag itself got nowhere.
;
; The driver treats a failure as "assume one display and carry on"
; (vc4_firmware_kms.c:1962-1970, "assuming old firmware that doesn't
; have the mailbox call"). This file does NOT fold that assumption into
; the return value, because a caller printing a number should not be
; handed a guess wearing the same clothes as a measurement. Callers that
; want the driver's fallback can write it themselves in one line.
;
; CACHED after the first successful answer. The firmware probes displays
; at boot and this cannot change while we run, and the monitor asks for
; it on a path where a mailbox round trip per report is pure cost.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DisplayCount()
  If dsp_ndisp >= 0
    ProcedureReturn dsp_ndisp
  EndIf
  ; The slot has to have been filled. This is the one place the
  ; not-required slots differ from the required ten in a way a caller
  ; can trip over: DisplayInit() refuses outright when a REQUIRED slot
  ; is empty, but these three are reachable before DisplayStandardTags()
  ; has ever run, and an empty slot holds zero - which is the END TAG.
  ; Sending it would compose a message whose first tag terminates it.
  If DisplayTagSet(#DSP_TAG_GETNUMDISPLAYS) = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If DspMailbox() = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ; A pure GET: no request words, one response word.
  If MailboxBegin(dsp_cfg[#DSP_TAG_GETNUMDISPLAYS], 0, 4) = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If MailboxSend() = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  dsp_ndisp = MailboxWord(0)
  ; A firmware that answered with rubbish is a firmware that did not
  ; answer. Nine is the length of the DispmanX id table the driver
  ; carries (vc4_firmware_kms.c:343-356) and nothing can present more
  ; displays than there are ids for them.
  If dsp_ndisp < 0 Or dsp_ndisp > 9
    dsp_ndisp = -1
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn dsp_ndisp
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayIdAt - the DispmanX ID of the display at one INDEX.
;
; index in, id out, THROUGH THE SAME WORD - that is the tag's shape, not
; a convenience of this wrapper (vc4_firmware_kms.c:1991-1994 writes the
; index into the value word and reads the id back out of it).
;
; Returns the id - 2 is HDMI0 and 7 is HDMI1, see #MBX_FB_DISPLAY_HDMI0
; in mailbox.pi4 for the whole table - or -1 if the firmware would not
; say. A negative index is refused here rather than sent: what the
; firmware does with an out-of-range index is not established by any
; source on this disk, and finding out by trying it is exactly the habit
; this project keeps paying for.
;
; NOT CACHED, unlike the count. One id per index would need an array and
; an invalidation rule, and the only caller asks once per report.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DisplayIdAt(index.i)
  If index < 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ; See DisplayCount() for why an unfilled slot is refused rather than
  ; sent: slot zero holds zero, and zero is the end tag.
  If DisplayTagSet(#DSP_TAG_GETDISPLAYID) = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If DspMailbox() = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If MailboxBegin(dsp_cfg[#DSP_TAG_GETDISPLAYID], 4, 4) = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  MailboxSetWord(0, index)
  If MailboxSend() = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn MailboxWord(0)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplaySelect - choose which display the NEXT DisplayInit() sets up.
;
; Returns 1 if the index was accepted, 0 if it was refused, and on a
; refusal leaves #DSP_ESELINDEX in DisplayLastError().
;
; IT DOES NOT TALK TO THE FRAMEBUFFER AND IT DOES NOT BRING A SCREEN UP.
; All it does is arm the selection tag. That is deliberate: the tag has
; to travel WITH the framebuffer message rather than ahead of it (see
; THE ONE THING NO SOURCE SETTLES above), so there is nothing useful to
; send at the moment the choice is made. A caller selects and then
; calls DisplayInit(); the monitor's "screen on 1" is exactly that pair.
;
; IT DOES CHECK THE INDEX AGAINST THE FIRMWARE, which costs one mailbox
; round trip and is worth it: an index past the end is the single most
; likely mistake here - somebody types the socket number 1 meaning
; HDMI1 on a board where only one display is attached, and the only
; valid index is 0. Being told so immediately is far better than a
; DisplayInit() that fails later for a reason that names a tag.
;
; If the firmware will not say how many displays it has, the index is
; ACCEPTED rather than refused. Refusing would make a firmware too old
; to answer one question unable to answer a different one, and the
; driver's own reaction to that silence is to carry on
; (vc4_firmware_kms.c:1962-1970).
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DisplaySelect(index.i)
  Define n.i
  If index < 0
    dsp_err = #DSP_ESELINDEX
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; No tag, no selection. DisplayInit() checks the ten REQUIRED slots
  ; and would not look at this one, so if it is refused anywhere it has
  ; to be refused here - a selection armed against an empty slot would
  ; put the end tag at the head of the framebuffer message and truncate
  ; it to nothing.
  If DisplayTagSet(#DSP_TAG_SETDISPLAYNUM) = 0
    dsp_err = #DSP_ENOTAGS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  n = DisplayCount()
  If n >= 0 And index >= n
    dsp_err = #DSP_ESELINDEX
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  dsp_sel = index
  ; The cached "which display are we on" belongs to the OLD selection.
  dsp_actid = -1
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplaySelectNone - go back to letting the firmware choose.
;
; After this, DisplayInit() sends exactly the traffic it sent before any
; of this existed. Kept as a procedure rather than "DisplaySelect(-1)"
; because -1 is a sentinel this file goes out of its way not to let a
; caller confuse with an index.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DisplaySelectNone()
  dsp_sel = -1
  dsp_actid = -1
EndProcedure

; DisplaySelected - the INDEX DisplaySelect() is holding, or -1 for
; "nobody chose and the firmware's pick stands".
Procedure.i DisplaySelected()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_sel
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayActiveId - the DispmanX ID of the display we are on, or -1.
;
; -1 IS A REAL AND COMMON ANSWER, and it is the honest one. There is no
; tag anywhere on this disk that asks the firmware which display the
; framebuffer ended up on. So:
;
;   a selection was made      -> the id of that index. We know because
;                                we chose it and every framebuffer
;                                message carried the choice.
;   no selection, ONE display -> the id of index 0. Not a guess: with
;                                one display the firmware had nothing
;                                else to pick.
;   no selection, more than
;   one display               -> -1. Say so. The old code's habit of
;                                naming HDMI0 here is the defect this
;                                whole section exists to end.
;
; Cached, because the two branches that can answer both cost a mailbox
; round trip and neither answer can change while the program runs.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DisplayActiveId()
  Define n.i
  If dsp_actid >= 0
    ProcedureReturn dsp_actid
  EndIf
  If dsp_sel >= 0
    dsp_actid = DisplayIdAt(dsp_sel)
    ProcedureReturn dsp_actid
  EndIf
  n = DisplayCount()
  If n = 1
    dsp_actid = DisplayIdAt(0)
    ProcedureReturn dsp_actid
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayInit - ask the firmware for a framebuffer.
;
; TWO MESSAGES. A QUERY, THEN ONE MESSAGE CARRYING NINE TAGS. Rewritten
; 2026-08-26; the shape before that was seven messages of one tag each,
; and what it produced on the direct-firmware boot path was a 2 x 2
; framebuffer with a pitch of 64 in front of a 1920 x 1080 panel. THE
; MAILBOX CONTRACT in the header has the diagnosis; this comment has the
; protocol.
;
;   MESSAGE 1   get physical width and height, alone
;
;               What mode is the panel in RIGHT NOW? Not what can it do,
;               not what would we like - what has the firmware already
;               settled on. This is U-Boot's bcm2835_get_video_size(),
;               v2025.01_msg.c:140-157, and it is a whole message of its
;               own because its answer is an INPUT to the next one.
;
;   MESSAGE 2   set physical w/h        the mode
;               set virtual w/h         the surface backing it
;               set depth               bits per pixel
;               set pixel order         which channel is which
;               set alpha mode          what the fourth byte means
;               set virtual offset      no pan
;               set overscan            zero on all four edges
;               ALLOCATE BUFFER         alignment $100
;               get pitch               bytes per row
;
;               In that order, which is U-Boot's struct msg_setup
;               (v2025.01_msg.c:36-45) filled by
;               bcm2835_set_video_params() (:166-189).
;
; AND ONE TAG IN FRONT OF EACH, IF A DISPLAY WAS CHOSEN. When - and only
; when - DisplaySelect() has been called, both messages gain
; set-display-num as their FIRST tag, carrying the chosen index. Message
; 1 becomes two tags, message 2 becomes ten. The nine are untouched, in
; the same order, with the same values; the selection sits in front of
; them and steers the whole message. With no selection made, neither
; message changes in any way at all. The argument for putting it in both
; rather than sending it once is in DISPLAY SELECTION above and it comes
; down to this: no source on this disk says whether the selection
; survives the end of a message, and leading with it is correct whether
; it does or not.
;
; WHY ONE MESSAGE AND NOT NINE - the sentence the old design was missing:
; THE FIRMWARE ALLOCATES AGAINST THE STATE ACCUMULATED WITHIN A SINGLE
; PROPERTY TRANSACTION. Separate messages do not build on one another
; for this purpose. Send set-depth in its own message and then allocate
; in another and the allocate does not see it.
;
; WHY THE QUERY FIRST, and this is the half that is easy to leave out:
; the size the combined message ASKS FOR is the size the query just
; reported. U-Boot does that (msg.c:168-172 feeds *widthp / *heightp,
; which its caller filled from bcm2835_get_video_size()), and it is not
; a courtesy to the panel - a request for a mode the firmware has not
; settled is what the placeholder answer comes back to. The width and
; height this procedure was CALLED with are the fallback for when the
; query will not answer, not the primary. What DisplayWidth() reports
; afterwards is, as it always was, the firmware's number and never the
; caller's wish.
;
; WHAT IS READ BACK, AND FROM WHERE - all of it out of the ONE response,
; which is the other half of the fix. v2025.01_msg.c:196-201:
;   physical width, height   the set-physical tag's own response words
;   pitch                    the get-pitch tag's response word
;   framebuffer address      the allocate tag's response word 0
;   framebuffer size         the allocate tag's response word 1
; Reading the pitch out of a SECOND message would be reading it from a
; different transaction, which is the bug this rewrite exists to remove.
;
; Returns #DSP_OK, or one of the negative #DSP_E... codes, which is also
; left in DisplayLastError(). On a failure MailboxError() names the
; transport-level reason; and because mailbox.pi4 checks EVERY tag's own
; response bit and reports which one was not answered, a wrong tag
; number still surfaces as the code for exactly that tag.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; ======================================================================
;  DspInstall - THE ONE PLACE A SURFACE BECOMES THIS FILE'S SURFACE.
;
;  Both ways of getting a framebuffer end here: DisplayInit, which asks
;  the VideoCore firmware for one, and DisplayAdopt, which is handed one
;  the caller composed itself. Everything below this line - the depth it
;  can address, the one-cell floor, the pitch, the allocated size, the
;  font, the console geometry - is a property of the SURFACE and not of
;  where it came from, so it is checked and set once, here.
;
;  It used to be the tail of DisplayInit, which was fine while the
;  mailbox was the only source and became wrong the moment it was not:
;  a surface arriving any other way would have skipped every structural
;  check in the file.
;
;  `bus` is the raw VideoCore address the firmware returned, or 0 when
;  nothing handed one over; it is reported and never used for drawing.
;  `size` is the bytes known to be there.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i DspInstall(base.i, bus.i, size.i, pitch.i, w.i, h.i, depth.i)
  Define bpp.i

  ; The firmware is allowed to give a depth other than the one asked
  ; for. If it gave one this file cannot address, that is a refusal, not
  ; something to paper over by pretending the request won.
  If depth = 32
    bpp = 4
  ElseIf depth = 16
    bpp = 2
  Else
    dsp_err = #DSP_EDEPTH
    ProcedureReturn #DSP_EDEPTH
  EndIf

  ; --- the structural backstop ----------------------------------------
  ; mailbox.pi4 has already refused any tag the firmware did not
  ; acknowledge, so on that path these are the second line rather than
  ; the first. They are still worth having: they catch a firmware that
  ; acknowledged a tag and answered it with something unusable, which no
  ; response bit can detect - and on the adopted path they are the ONLY
  ; line, because nobody else looked at the numbers at all.
  If w < 1 Or h < 1
    dsp_err = #DSP_EMODE
    ProcedureReturn #DSP_EMODE
  EndIf

  ; A SURFACE TOO SMALL FOR ONE CHARACTER IS NOT A SURFACE. Added
  ; 2026-08-26 as the backstop for the failure the single-transaction
  ; rewrite exists for: the seven-message shape returned a
  ; plausible-looking success over a 2 x 2 framebuffer,
  ; DisplayConsoleFull() then computed a negative number of rows, and
  ; the caller had no way to tell that from a working display until
  ; something drew on it.
  ;
  ; The floor is one cell of the built-in font, 8 x 16, and it is that
  ; rather than a round number because it is the smallest surface on
  ; which anything this file offers can be done at all. It is not a
  ; judgement about what makes a usable screen - callers make that
  ; judgement themselves and the monitor's is far stricter - it is the
  ; line below which the geometry stops being arithmetic and starts
  ; being nonsense.
  If w < 8 Or h < 16
    dsp_err = #DSP_EMODE
    ProcedureReturn #DSP_EMODE
  EndIf

  If base = 0
    dsp_err = #DSP_ENOBUFFER
    ProcedureReturn #DSP_ENOBUFFER
  EndIf
  If pitch = 0 Or pitch < (w * bpp)
    dsp_err = #DSP_EPITCH
    ProcedureReturn #DSP_EPITCH
  EndIf
  If size < (pitch * h)
    dsp_err = #DSP_ESIZE
    ProcedureReturn #DSP_ESIZE
  EndIf

  dsp_bus   = bus
  dsp_base  = base
  dsp_size  = size
  dsp_pitch = pitch
  dsp_viewOff = 0                ; a freshly installed surface shows its top
  dsp_w     = w
  dsp_h     = h
  dsp_depth = depth
  dsp_bpp   = bpp
  dsp_ready = 1
  dsp_err   = #DSP_OK

  DisplayFontDefault()
  dsp_fg = DisplayRGB(200, 200, 200)
  dsp_bg = DisplayRGB(0, 0, 0)
  DisplayConsoleFull()

  ProcedureReturn #DSP_OK
EndProcedure


Procedure.i DisplayInit(width.i, height.i, depth.i)
  Define aw.i
  Define ah.i
  Define ad.i
  Define rw.i
  Define rh.i
  Define bus.i
  Define sz.i
  Define pitch.i
  Define bpp.i
  Define s.i
  Define rc.i
  Define selix.i               ; where set-display-num landed in the list
  Define qix.i                 ; ...and where the get-physical query did
  Define want.i                ; how many tags message 2 should hold

  dsp_ready = 0
  dsp_base = 0
  dsp_panelw = 0
  dsp_panelh = 0
  ; Clear the index table before composing. A stale index left from a
  ; previous call could match the failed tag of this one and name the
  ; wrong step, which is worse than naming none.
  s = 0
  While s < #DSP_SLOT_COUNT
    dsp_tix[s] = -1
    s = s + 1
  Wend

  If DisplayTagsReady() = 0
    dsp_err = #DSP_ENOTAGS
    ProcedureReturn #DSP_ENOTAGS
  EndIf
  If width < 1 Or height < 1
    dsp_err = #DSP_EARGS
    ProcedureReturn #DSP_EARGS
  EndIf
  ; 24bpp has no aligned store and 8bpp needs a palette tag this file
  ; cannot cite. Refusing is the honest answer; see PIXEL FORMAT.
  If depth <> 32 And depth <> 16
    dsp_err = #DSP_EDEPTH
    ProcedureReturn #DSP_EDEPTH
  EndIf

  ; MailboxInit() is idempotent-ish but not free, so only call it if the
  ; transport is not already up. A main file that has already used the
  ; mailbox for a clock rate has done this.
  If MailboxReady() = 0
    If MailboxInit() = 0
      dsp_err = #DSP_ENOMAILBOX
      ProcedureReturn #DSP_ENOMAILBOX
    EndIf
  EndIf

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  MESSAGE 1 - what is the panel already doing?
  ;
  ;  A pure GET: no request words, two response words. U-Boot builds it
  ;  with BCM2835_MBOX_INIT_TAG_NO_REQ (v2025.01_msg.c:146-147) for the
  ;  same reason - there is nothing to send.
  ;
  ;  A FAILURE HERE IS NOT FATAL, and that is a deliberate choice rather
  ;  than an oversight. The caller passed a width and a height; if the
  ;  firmware will not say what it has, using the caller's numbers is
  ;  strictly better than refusing, and it is what this procedure did
  ;  for its whole life before today. It is not silent either: the
  ;  numbers used are the ones the caller can see it was given.
  ;
  ;  TWO SHAPES, AND THE FIRST ONE IS UNTOUCHED. With no display chosen
  ;  this is the single-tag message it has always been, sent through the
  ;  single-tag half of the transport, identical on the wire. With a
  ;  display chosen it becomes a two-tag list - set-display-num, then
  ;  the query - because ASKING THE WRONG SOCKET WHAT MODE IT IS IN is
  ;  the quiet way to get this wrong: the answer feeds the size the
  ;  nine-tag message then asks for, so a query aimed at HDMI0 and a
  ;  configuration aimed at HDMI1 would produce a completely successful
  ;  init at the other panel's geometry.
  ;
  ;  The two shapes are written out separately rather than folded into
  ;  one list-based path that happens to hold one tag when nothing is
  ;  selected. That would have been shorter and it would have changed
  ;  the bytes on the wire for every existing caller - the list path
  ;  writes U-Boot's value length in the tag header where the single-tag
  ;  path writes the kernel's zero (see ONE DELIBERATE DIFFERENCE in
  ;  mailbox.pi4). Both work; only one of them is the one this board has
  ;  been booting with, and this is not the change to find that out in.
  ; ==================================================================
  rw = width
  rh = height
  If dsp_sel >= 0
    If MailboxListBegin() <> 0
      selix = MailboxListAdd(dsp_cfg[#DSP_TAG_SETDISPLAYNUM], 4, 4)
      qix   = MailboxListAdd(dsp_cfg[#DSP_TAG_GETPHYS], 0, 8)
      If selix >= 0 And qix >= 0
        MailboxListSetWord(selix, 0, dsp_sel)
        If MailboxListSend() <> 0
          dsp_panelw = MailboxListWord(qix, 0)
          dsp_panelh = MailboxListWord(qix, 1)
        EndIf
      EndIf
    EndIf
  Else
    If DspStep(#DSP_TAG_GETPHYS, 0, 8) <> 0
      If MailboxSend() <> 0
        dsp_panelw = MailboxWord(0)
        dsp_panelh = MailboxWord(1)
      EndIf
    EndIf
  EndIf
  If dsp_panelw > 0 And dsp_panelh > 0
    rw = dsp_panelw
    rh = dsp_panelh
  EndIf

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  MESSAGE 2 - nine tags, one transaction
  ; ==================================================================
  If MailboxListBegin() = 0
    dsp_err = #DSP_ENOMAILBOX
    ProcedureReturn #DSP_ENOMAILBOX
  EndIf

  ; The value-buffer sizes are the C structs' request and response
  ; halves, tag by tag, from v2025.01_mbox.h:
  ;   physical_w_h   :317-330  req 8   resp 8
  ;   virtual_w_h    :337-350  req 8   resp 8
  ;   depth          :356-367  req 4   resp 4
  ;   pixel_order    :376-387  req 4   resp 4
  ;   alpha_mode     :397-408  req 4   resp 4
  ;   virtual_offset :428-441  req 8   resp 8
  ;   overscan       :447-464  req 16  resp 16
  ;   allocate       :272-283  req 4   resp 8
  ;   pitch          :412-421  req 0   resp 4
  ;
  ; ...and the tenth, which is not U-Boot's because U-Boot has no such
  ; tag. Four bytes, one word, the chosen INDEX. The value buffer size
  ; is the one the driver writes into the tag header by hand:
  ; vc4_firmware_kms.c:1846, "{SET_DISPLAY_NUM, 4, 0, }".
  ;
  ; IT GOES FIRST OR IT IS POINTLESS. The firmware processes tags in
  ; buffer order (see the note on MailboxListAdd in mailbox.pi4), and
  ; the driver's only use of this tag puts it ahead of the tag it
  ; steers. Ahead of ALL NINE, not merely ahead of the allocate: the
  ; setters are what the allocate reads, so a selection that arrived
  ; after them would at best select a display for a state that had been
  ; accumulated for another one.
  If dsp_sel >= 0
    DspAdd(#DSP_TAG_SETDISPLAYNUM, 4, 4)
  EndIf
  DspAdd(#DSP_TAG_SETPHYS,     8,  8)
  DspAdd(#DSP_TAG_SETVIRT,     8,  8)
  DspAdd(#DSP_TAG_SETDEPTH,    4,  4)
  DspAdd(#DSP_TAG_SETORDER,    4,  4)
  DspAdd(#DSP_TAG_SETALPHA,    4,  4)
  DspAdd(#DSP_TAG_SETOFFSET,   8,  8)
  DspAdd(#DSP_TAG_SETOVERSCAN, 16, 16)
  DspAdd(#DSP_TAG_ALLOCATE,    4,  8)
  DspAdd(#DSP_TAG_GETPITCH,    0,  4)

  ; Every one of them, or none. MailboxListAdd() returns -1 and refuses
  ; to write when the buffer is full, so a short list means the message
  ; would have gone out missing a tag - and a combined message missing a
  ; tag is precisely the failure this rewrite is about. Counting once is
  ; enough: the count cannot be nine unless all nine were accepted.
  ;
  ; TEN WHEN A DISPLAY WAS CHOSEN. The count is computed from the same
  ; condition that added the tag rather than being relaxed to "nine or
  ; ten", because "nine or ten" would accept a run that meant to select
  ; and silently did not.
  want = #DSP_MSG_TAGS
  If dsp_sel >= 0
    want = #DSP_MSG_TAGS + 1
  EndIf
  If MailboxListCount() <> want
    dsp_err = #DSP_ECOMPOSE
    ProcedureReturn #DSP_ECOMPOSE
  EndIf

  ; --- the display, if one was chosen --------------------------------
  ; The index, not the DispmanX id. vc4_firmware_kms.c:1847 passes
  ; display_idx, which is the 0..num_displays-1 loop counter, and NOT
  ; the display_ref it got back from GET_DISPLAY_ID two lines earlier
  ; (:1991-1994). The two numberings are set out in mailbox.pi4 under
  ; THE TWO NUMBERINGS; the short version is that with one display in
  ; HDMI1 the index is 0 and the id is 7.
  If dsp_sel >= 0
    MailboxListSetWord(dsp_tix[#DSP_TAG_SETDISPLAYNUM], 0, dsp_sel)
  EndIf

  ; --- the physical mode, and the virtual surface behind it ----------
  ; Both get the SAME size, which is U-Boot's choice too
  ; (v2025.01_msg.c:168-172). A virtual surface larger than the physical
  ; one is how hardware panning and double buffering are done and both
  ; are future work; a virtual size left over from a previous mode is a
  ; real way to get a picture the wrong size, which is why the tag is
  ; sent rather than left alone.
  MailboxListSetWord(dsp_tix[#DSP_TAG_SETPHYS], 0, rw)
  MailboxListSetWord(dsp_tix[#DSP_TAG_SETPHYS], 1, rh)
  MailboxListSetWord(dsp_tix[#DSP_TAG_SETVIRT], 0, rw)
  ; The VIRTUAL height is dsp_virtMul times the physical one - normally 1
  ; (no panning), but 2 when a page-flipping console wants an off-screen
  ; back buffer stacked below the visible one. The firmware allocates
  ; pitch * virtualHeight bytes, so ALLOCATE returns the double-size
  ; surface and GET_PITCH still returns the single-row stride.
  MailboxListSetWord(dsp_tix[#DSP_TAG_SETVIRT], 1, rh * dsp_virtMul)

  ; --- depth ---------------------------------------------------------
  ; U-Boot hardcodes 32 here (v2025.01_msg.c:174). This file sends what
  ; the caller asked for, because it supports 16 as well and refuses
  ; everything else up front. The firmware is still free to answer with
  ; something different and that answer is what gets believed.
  MailboxListSetWord(dsp_tix[#DSP_TAG_SETDEPTH], 0, depth)

  ; --- pixel order ---------------------------------------------------
  ; #MBX_FB_ORDER_BGR unless DisplayPixelOrder() said otherwise. The
  ; long note on DisplayStandardTags() is where the choice is argued;
  ; the short version is that BGR is what the bench measured on the
  ; direct-firmware boot and it is the arrangement DisplayRGB()'s
  ; packing already assumes.
  MailboxListSetWord(dsp_tix[#DSP_TAG_SETORDER], 0, dsp_order)

  ; --- alpha mode ----------------------------------------------------
  ; #MBX_FB_ALPHA_IGNORED, and the choice between the three matters more
  ; than it looks. v2025.01_mbox.h:393-395 gives them as
  ;   0  alpha 0 means OPAQUE
  ;   1  alpha 0 means TRANSPARENT
  ;   2  the alpha channel is IGNORED
  ; DisplayRGB() puts $FF in the top byte of every 32bpp colour, which
  ; is right under XRGB and means "opaque" under ARGB. Under mode 0 that
  ; $FF would be the far end of the scale from zero-means-opaque and
  ; every pixel this library draws would be at the wrong end of it. Mode
  ; 2 makes the byte mean nothing at all, which is the only one of the
  ; three that cannot contradict what DisplayRGB() writes.
  ;
  ; U-Boot takes this value from its caller and that caller is not on
  ; this disk, so this is NOT "what U-Boot does" - it is a choice made
  ; here, from the cited meanings, and it is written out so the next
  ; person can disagree with the reasoning rather than with a number.
  MailboxListSetWord(dsp_tix[#DSP_TAG_SETALPHA], 0, #MBX_FB_ALPHA_IGNORED)

  ; --- virtual offset, (0,0) -----------------------------------------
  ; v2025.01_msg.c:180-181. No pan. Sent rather than assumed because an
  ; offset left behind by a previous mode shows the wrong part of the
  ; surface and nothing reports it.
  MailboxListSetWord(dsp_tix[#DSP_TAG_SETOFFSET], 0, 0)
  MailboxListSetWord(dsp_tix[#DSP_TAG_SETOFFSET], 1, 0)

  ; --- overscan, zero on all four edges ------------------------------
  ; v2025.01_msg.c:183-186, top / bottom / left / right, in that order
  ; (v2025.01_mbox.h:452-455). U-Boot sets all four to zero EXPLICITLY
  ; and this file does the same.
  ;
  ; WHY WRITE FOUR ZEROS THAT MailboxListAdd() HAS ALREADY WRITTEN. Two
  ; reasons and neither is style. The first is that overscan is state
  ; the firmware carries from wherever it was left - config.txt, a
  ; previous mode, a bootloader - and "zero" is a value being commanded
  ; here, not a value being defaulted to. The second is that a request
  ; whose meaning depends on a zero-fill somewhere else is a request
  ; nobody can read. The bench symptom of overscan left alone is a
  ; picture inset from the edges of the screen with a black border, and
  ; on this board the firmware applies it of its own accord - 1824 x 984
  ; out of a 1920 x 1080 panel was what U-Boot's own path produced.
  MailboxListSetWord(dsp_tix[#DSP_TAG_SETOVERSCAN], 0, 0)
  MailboxListSetWord(dsp_tix[#DSP_TAG_SETOVERSCAN], 1, 0)
  MailboxListSetWord(dsp_tix[#DSP_TAG_SETOVERSCAN], 2, 0)
  MailboxListSetWord(dsp_tix[#DSP_TAG_SETOVERSCAN], 3, 0)

  ; --- allocate ------------------------------------------------------
  ; The one request word is the alignment wanted. $100 is U-Boot's
  ; number (v2025.01_msg.c:188) and there is no reason to differ; the
  ; 4096 an earlier draft asked for was this file's own choice and it
  ; was never a protocol constant.
  MailboxListSetWord(dsp_tix[#DSP_TAG_ALLOCATE], 0, #MBX_FB_ALLOC_ALIGN)

  ; --- get pitch -----------------------------------------------------
  ; No request words. It is LAST in the message, after the allocate, for
  ; the same reason the allocate is after the setters: the pitch is a
  ; property of the surface the allocate just created. Being in the same
  ; message is what makes "just created" true.

  If MailboxListSend() = 0
    ; #MBX_ERR_TAG means the firmware understood the buffer and refused
    ; one tag in it; anything else means the transaction itself failed
    ; and no tag is to blame.
    If MailboxError() = #MBX_ERR_TAG
      rc = DspFailedCode()
    Else
      rc = #DSP_ESEND
    EndIf
    dsp_err = rc
    ProcedureReturn rc
  EndIf

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  EVERYTHING OUT OF THAT ONE RESPONSE
  ; ==================================================================
  aw    = MailboxListWord(dsp_tix[#DSP_TAG_SETPHYS], 0)
  ah    = MailboxListWord(dsp_tix[#DSP_TAG_SETPHYS], 1)
  ad    = MailboxListWord(dsp_tix[#DSP_TAG_SETDEPTH], 0)
  bus   = MailboxListWord(dsp_tix[#DSP_TAG_ALLOCATE], 0)
  sz    = MailboxListWord(dsp_tix[#DSP_TAG_ALLOCATE], 1)
  pitch = MailboxListWord(dsp_tix[#DSP_TAG_GETPITCH], 0)

  ; EVERY CHECK ON THE SHAPE OF WHAT CAME BACK NOW LIVES IN DspInstall,
  ; because they are checks on a SURFACE and not on the mailbox: the
  ; depth this file can address, the one-cell floor, the pitch, the
  ; allocated size. They used to sit here, which meant a surface that
  ; did not arrive through the firmware would have been installed
  ; unchecked. See DspInstall.

  ; BUS TO ARM. bcm2711.dtsi:45 pins bus $C0000000 to ARM $00000000 for
  ; $40000000 bytes, so the two alias-selector bits come off and the low
  ; 30 bits are the offset into DRAM. See THE FRAMEBUFFER POINTER IS A
  ; BUS ADDRESS in the header for the two ways this could be wrong and
  ; why DisplayBusBase() reports the raw value as well.
  ;
  ; mailbox.pi4 converts the other way, ARM + $C0000000, for the buffer
  ; it sends. Same dma-ranges entry, same gigabyte, opposite direction.
  ; U-Boot calls its version bus_to_phys() (v2025.01_msg.c:199).
  ProcedureReturn DspInstall(bus & $3FFFFFFF, bus, sz, pitch, aw, ah, ad)
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  DisplayAdopt - BE GIVEN A SURFACE INSTEAD OF ASKING THE FIRMWARE FOR
;  ONE.
;
;  WHY THIS EXISTS, AND WHY IT IS NOT A SECOND PATH BOLTED ON BESIDE
;  DisplayInit. This file used to conflate two different jobs in one
;  procedure: ACQUIRING a framebuffer from the VideoCore firmware, and
;  INSTALLING one so the drawing primitives can use it. Only the first
;  of those has anything to do with the mailbox. Every primitive in this
;  file draws through dsp_base, dsp_pitch, dsp_w, dsp_h and dsp_bpp, and
;  not one of them cares where those five numbers came from.
;
;  The Pi 4's DSI panel is a framebuffer NOTHING can ask the firmware
;  for. The firmware never brings DSI up - its DSI1 power domain is off
;  at boot and it owns no DSI display - so there is no display to select
;  and no allocation to request. The monitor composes that surface
;  itself: it owns the memory, programs an HVS display list over it and
;  points pixel valve 1 at the panel. What it needs from this file is
;  exactly the second job and none of the first.
;
;  So the second job was given its own name and ONE implementation -
;  DspInstall - with two ways in: DisplayInit asks the firmware and
;  installs the answer, DisplayAdopt installs an answer the caller
;  already has. There is no duplicated arithmetic, no flag, and no
;  branch inside any primitive.
;
;  THE CALLER OWNS THE MEMORY. This file never frees a surface and never
;  did - the firmware's allocation is not handed back either - so
;  adopting one changes nothing about lifetime. What the caller must
;  guarantee is that the bytes exist, that there are at least
;  pitch * height of them, and that nothing else in the memory map
;  claims them.
;
;  Returns #DSP_OK, or the same negative code DisplayInit would return
;  for the same defect, so one reporter prints both.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i DisplayAdopt(base.i, pitch.i, w.i, h.i, depth.i)
  If base = 0
    dsp_err = #DSP_ENOBUFFER
    ProcedureReturn #DSP_ENOBUFFER
  EndIf
  If w < 1 Or h < 1
    dsp_err = #DSP_EARGS
    ProcedureReturn #DSP_EARGS
  EndIf
  If pitch < 1
    dsp_err = #DSP_EPITCH
    ProcedureReturn #DSP_EPITCH
  EndIf
  ; An adopted surface has no VideoCore bus address to report, because
  ; nothing handed one over. DisplayBusBase() answers 0 for it, which is
  ; the truthful answer rather than an alias nobody computed.
  ProcedureReturn DspInstall(base, 0, pitch * h, pitch, w, h, depth)
EndProcedure


; DisplayPanelWidth / DisplayPanelHeight - what the panel said it was
; ALREADY doing, before this file told it anything, or 0 if the query
; would not answer. Reported because the difference between that and
; DisplayWidth() is the whole of what the configuration step did, and
; because a zero here is the one thing that turns the caller's own
; width and height from a fallback into the request that was actually
; made.
Procedure.i DisplayPanelWidth()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_panelw
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DisplayPanelHeight()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_panelh
EndProcedure


Procedure.i DisplayLastError()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_err
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DisplayReady()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_ready
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DisplayWidth()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_w
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DisplayHeight()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_h
EndProcedure

; DisplayPitch - BYTES from the start of one row to the start of the
; next, as the firmware reported it. Not width*bpp. See the header.
Procedure.i DisplayPitch()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_pitch
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DisplayDepth()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_depth
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DisplayBytesPerPixel()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_bpp
EndProcedure

; DisplayBase - ARM physical address of pixel (0,0).
Procedure.i DisplayBase()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_base
EndProcedure

; DisplaySetBase - repoint the origin the drawing primitives write to.
; Needed by the V3D console, which CPU-blits the banner into the OFF-SCREEN
; back half (the one about to be flipped in), not the front half. Every
; primitive here writes to dsp_base, so aiming it at the back half and
; putting it back is all it takes. THE CALLER MUST SAVE AND RESTORE - the
; DMA console and everything else read this; it is not for general use.
Procedure DisplaySetBase(base.i)
  dsp_base = base
EndProcedure

; DisplayBusBase - the raw VideoCore bus address, before the alias bits
; were stripped. Reported so a bring-up program can print both and see
; the conversion rather than trust it.
Procedure.i DisplayBusBase()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_bus
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DisplaySize()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_size
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  DOUBLE BUFFERING / VSYNC PAGE FLIP
;
;  A single framebuffer drawn while the display scans it out TEARS. The
;  cure the firmware offers is a virtual surface taller than the physical
;  one and the SET_VIRTUAL_OFFSET tag ($00048009), which it applies at the
;  vertical blank: allocate two screens stacked (DisplaySetVirtMul(2)
;  before DisplayInit), draw the next frame into the half that is NOT being
;  shown, then DisplayFlip() to it. The flip lands at vblank, so the shown
;  buffer is always a complete frame - a true page flip, no tear, no copy.
;
;  dsp_viewOff tracks which half is live so DisplayViewBase() (and the
;  screenshot that uses it) reads the half on the glass, not always the
;  top one.
; ======================================================================

; DisplaySetVirtMul - ask the NEXT DisplayInit for a virtual surface this
; many times the physical height (1 or 2). Inert until DisplayInit runs.
Procedure DisplaySetVirtMul(m.i)
  If m < 1
    m = 1
  EndIf
  dsp_virtMul = m
EndProcedure

; DisplayVirtualHeight - the allocated surface's height in pixels, which
; is dsp_virtMul physical screens when the double buffer took. Derived
; from the firmware's own allocated size and pitch, not from the request,
; so it reports what was actually granted.
Procedure.i DisplayVirtualHeight()
  If dsp_pitch < 1
    ProcedureReturn dsp_h
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn dsp_size / dsp_pitch
EndProcedure

; DisplayViewOffset - the byte offset within the buffer of the origin the
; firmware is currently scanning out (0 for the top half).
Procedure.i DisplayViewOffset()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_viewOff
EndProcedure

; DisplayViewBase - the ARM address of the pixel (0,0) that is ON THE
; GLASS right now, i.e. DisplayBase() plus the live view offset. A caller
; that reads the framebuffer to photograph it (CmdPic) uses this so it
; captures the half being displayed after a page flip.
Procedure.i DisplayViewBase()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_base + dsp_viewOff
EndProcedure

; DisplayFlip - present the half whose top row is `y` pixels down the
; virtual surface (0 = top half, DisplayHeight() = bottom half). Issues
; SET_VIRTUAL_OFFSET, which the firmware applies at the next vertical
; blank. Returns 1 if the flip was accepted. Records the new live offset.
Procedure.i DisplayFlip(y.i)
  If y < 0
    y = 0
  EndIf
  ; The tag is not one mailbox.pi4 wraps; it is issued at the call site
  ; through the single-tag path, exactly as ScreenPixelOrder does. Two
  ; value words: x offset (always 0) and y offset in pixels.
  If MailboxBegin($00048009, 8, 8) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  MailboxSetWord(0, 0)
  MailboxSetWord(1, y)
  If MailboxSend() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  dsp_viewOff = y * dsp_pitch
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  COLOUR
; ======================================================================

; DisplayColourSwap - 1 if red and blue come out the wrong way round on
; the actual monitor. This is the one thing in the file that only an eye
; can settle; see PIXEL FORMAT in the header.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayPixelOrder - the VALUE to send with the optional set-pixel-order
; tag. Only used if #DSP_TAG_SETORDER was given a tag number.
;
; The value's meaning - which number is RGB and which is BGR - is
; firmware documentation exactly like the tag number, and is not on this
; disk either, so it is not written down here. It is a separate control
; from DisplayColourSwap(): this one asks the FIRMWARE to lay the
; channels out differently, that one changes how THIS FILE packs them.
; If you have no verified tag number, use DisplayColourSwap() and ignore
; this; it needs nothing from the firmware and costs nothing.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DisplayPixelOrder(v.i)
  dsp_order = v & $FFFFFFFF
EndProcedure

Procedure DisplayColourSwap(on.i)
  If on <> 0
    dsp_swap = 1
  Else
    dsp_swap = 0
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayRGB - pack three 0..255 channels into a pixel of the current
; depth. Shifts are parenthesised throughout: & and | bind TIGHTER than
; << on this compiler, so an unparenthesised "r << 16 | g << 8 | b"
; would parse as "r << (16 | g) << (8 | b)".
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DisplayRGB(r.i, g.i, b.i)
  Define rr.i
  Define gg.i
  Define bb.i
  rr = r & $FF
  gg = g & $FF
  bb = b & $FF
  If dsp_swap = 1
    rr = b & $FF
    bb = r & $FF
  EndIf
  If dsp_bpp = 2
    ; 5:6:5. The low bits of each channel are discarded, which is what
    ; makes a smooth gradient band at 16bpp - a property of the mode,
    ; not of this code.
    ProcedureReturn (((rr >> 3) << 11) | ((gg >> 2) << 5)) | (bb >> 3)
  EndIf
  ; 32bpp. The top byte is $FF: ignored if the mode is XRGB, opaque if
  ; it is ARGB. Correct under both; leaving it clear is wrong under one.
  ProcedureReturn ((($FF << 24) | (rr << 16)) | (gg << 8)) | bb
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  CACHE COHERENCY
;
;  See CACHE COHERENCY in the header for why a framebuffer needs this
;  and an SPI panel does not.
; ======================================================================

Procedure DisplayAutoFlush(on.i)
  If on <> 0
    dsp_auto = 1
  Else
    dsp_auto = 0
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayCacheLine - clean the one line at dsp_flushaddr to the Point of
; Coherency.
;
; cvac, not cvau: the reader is the display controller, so this is the
; DMA case. anvil.pi4 uses cvau because its reader is the
; instruction fetcher, and its header carries the encoding table these
; come from. "dc cvac, x9" assembles to $D50B7A29 - checked against the
; bytes of the emitted image, not against the table.
;
; "ldr x9", not "ldr w9". dsp_flushaddr is a .i and a .i is EIGHT bytes
; on this target; ldr w9 would load only the low half of the address,
; which assembles cleanly, uses the right register, and silently drops
; the top 32 bits. This is the shape of bug the widening creates in
; hand-written asm and it is the reason this block is three lines of
; address materialisation for one instruction of work.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DisplayCacheLine()
  ASM
    adrp x9, global_dsp_flushaddr
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_dsp_flushaddr
    ldr  x9, [x9]
    dc cvac, x9
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayFlushRect - clean the bytes that rectangle covers.
;
; ROW BY ROW, because the rectangle is not contiguous in memory: it is h
; runs of w*bpp bytes, each dsp_pitch apart. Cleaning the whole span
; from the first byte to the last would also clean everything to the
; left and right of the rectangle on every row, which is correct but can
; be many times the work on a narrow column.
;
; 64 bytes is the Cortex-A72 line, the same constant anvil.pi4
; uses. The start is rounded DOWN to a line boundary with a shift pair
; rather than a mask, for the 64-bit reason in the header.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DisplayFlushRect(x.i, y.i, w.i, h.i)
  Define x0.i
  Define y0.i
  Define x1.i
  Define y1.i
  Define row.i
  Define a.i
  Define last.i

  If dsp_ready = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  x0 = x
  y0 = y
  x1 = x + w
  y1 = y + h
  If x0 < 0 : x0 = 0 : EndIf
  If y0 < 0 : y0 = 0 : EndIf
  If x1 > dsp_w : x1 = dsp_w : EndIf
  If y1 > dsp_h : y1 = dsp_h : EndIf
  If x1 <= x0 Or y1 <= y0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  row = y0
  While row < y1
    a    = ((dsp_base + (row * dsp_pitch)) + (x0 * dsp_bpp))
    last = a + ((x1 - x0) * dsp_bpp) - 1
    a = (a >> 6) << 6
    While a <= last
      dsp_flushaddr = a
      DisplayCacheLine()
      a = a + 64
    Wend
    row = row + 1
  Wend

  ; One barrier for the whole rectangle. dsb sy + isb, from
  ; RaspberryPi4/Intrinsics/bcm2711_hardware.def - an intrinsic, not a
  ; library, so this is not an undeclared dependency.
  a64_barrier()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DspFlushSpan - clean ONE CONTIGUOUS run of bytes, lo .. lo+n-1.
;
; Internal, added 2026-08-26 with the console scroll rewrite. It is not
; a faster DisplayFlushRect and it must not be used as one: it has no
; idea what a row is, so handing it a narrow rectangle would clean
; everything to the left and right of it on every row - which is the
; exact waste the row-by-row note on DisplayFlushRect exists to avoid.
;
; It is for the ONE case where that note does not apply: a region that
; really is contiguous in memory, which on this surface means a whole
; number of FULL rows with pitch = width * bpp. The console scroll
; produces exactly that and nothing else in this file does.
;
; The win is not the arithmetic - DisplayFlushRect's per-row setup is
; noise next to 96,672 cache lines. It is that the caller can hand over
; the whole 6.2 MB block once and get ONE barrier for it, instead of
; being tempted to call DisplayFlushRect per row and pay 848 of them.
;
; 64 bytes is the Cortex-A72 line, as above. The start is rounded DOWN
; with a shift pair rather than a mask for the 64-bit reason in the
; header: "lo & $FFFFFFC0" clears the top half of the address too.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DspFlushSpan(lo.i, n.i)
  Define a.i
  Define last.i
  If n < 1
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  last = lo + n - 1
  a = (lo >> 6) << 6
  While a <= last
    dsp_flushaddr = a
    DisplayCacheLine()
    a = a + 64
  Wend
  a64_barrier()
EndProcedure

Procedure DisplayFlush()
  DisplayFlushRect(0, 0, dsp_w, dsp_h)
EndProcedure

; Internal: honour the auto-flush setting after a primitive has drawn.
;
; IT IS THE CPU PATH'S FLUSH AND ONLY THE CPU PATH'S. Nothing on the DMA
; side calls it; see step 4 of the sequence in CACHE COHERENCY WITH A BUS
; MASTER, which says why cleaning after a hardware transfer is at best
; wasted and at worst destructive.
Procedure DspTouched(x.i, y.i, w.i, h.i)
  If dsp_auto = 1
    DisplayFlushRect(x, y, w, h)
  EndIf
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  THE DMA SEAM
;
;  Everything in this section that TOUCHES the engine is inside
;  CompilerIf #DSP_DMA_LINKED = 1 and vanishes at the token level
;  otherwise. Everything that REPORTS on it is outside, so a program can
;  always ask and always get a truthful answer.
;
;  Read THE DMA SEAM and CACHE COHERENCY WITH A BUS MASTER in the header
;  before changing one line of this.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayDmaLinked - was dma.pi4 compiled into this build?
;
; A COMPILE-TIME FACT ANSWERED AT RUN TIME, on purpose. A bring-up
; program prints it, and a screen that says "dma: not linked" is a
; complete diagnosis of a build that was expected to have it. The
; alternative - the caller repeating #DSP_DMA_LINKED in its own printout
; - is the caller asserting what it asked for rather than what it got,
; and the two differ exactly when it matters.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DisplayDmaLinked()
  CompilerIf #DSP_DMA_LINKED = 1
    ProcedureReturn 1
  CompilerElse
    ProcedureReturn 0
  CompilerEndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayUseDma - the RUN switch. Off by default even when linked.
;
; TWO SWITCHES AND NOT ONE. Linking is a build decision; using is a run
; decision. The reason to keep them apart is the bench: when a picture
; comes out wrong the first question is "which path drew it", and
; answering that must not require a rebuild and a reflash. uart.pi4's
; mirror is off by default for the same reason (:485-489).
;
; IT REFUSES WHEN NOTHING IS LINKED rather than pretending. Returning 1
; from a call that did nothing is how a program comes to believe it is
; measuring the engine while it measures the CPU, and that belief is
; very hard to shake once it is in a log file.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DisplayUseDma(on.i)
  CompilerIf #DSP_DMA_LINKED = 1
    If on <> 0
      dsp_dmaOn = 1
    Else
      dsp_dmaOn = 0
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn 1
  CompilerElse
    ; NOT LINKED. The flag is forced to zero rather than left alone,
    ; because "the caller asked for the engine" and "the flag says the
    ; engine is on" must never be able to disagree, and this branch is
    ; the one where they could. The argument is ignored on purpose; the
    ; return value is the whole answer.
    dsp_dmaOn = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  CompilerEndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayDmaReadback - keep the post-transfer invalidate, or drop it.
;
; ON BY DEFAULT. It is hazard B in the header: the engine wrote DRAM and
; the CPU may hold, or may speculatively acquire, a clean line over those
; addresses, so every later CPU read of the rectangle returns the old
; picture until the line is invalidated.
;
; TURNING IT OFF IS LEGAL AND IS NOT A MICRO-OPTIMISATION - it is one
; "dc civac" per 64 bytes over the whole rectangle, which on a full
; 8.29 MB surface is 129,600 operations. It is correct ONLY for a
; program that never reads the surface back with the CPU: no
; DisplayGetPixel, no CPU-path DisplayCopyRect, no CPU-path scroll, no
; glyph renderer reading its own background. A compositor that only ever
; writes qualifies. A console does not, because its scroll reads.
;
; IT IS NOT AUTOMATIC AND MUST NOT BECOME AUTOMATIC. This file cannot
; know what the caller will do next, and a library that guessed would be
; guessing about the hazard whose symptom appears furthest from its
; cause.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DisplayDmaReadback(on.i)
  If on <> 0
    dsp_dmaRead = 1
  Else
    dsp_dmaRead = 0
  EndIf
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DisplayDmaReadbackGet()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_dmaRead
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayDmaMin - the area, in PIXELS, below which the CPU is used even
; when the engine is available and switched on.
;
; ======================================================================
; THIS DEFAULT IS NOT MEASURED AND THE NUMBER SAYS SO
; ======================================================================
; The crossover cannot be computed today, because ONE OF THE TWO NUMBERS
; DOES NOT EXIST YET. The CPU side is measured: 266.4 ms for 1,794,816
; pixels is about 148 ns a pixel (damage.pi4:54-68). The DMA side is
; NOT - RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/pi4DmaProbe.pi4 has never been
; run, so neither the engine's throughput nor, more importantly, the
; FIXED cost of one submission is known on this part.
;
; What a submission costs is a list, not a guess:
;   * 64 PokeL stores to lay the 256-byte pattern down (dma.pi4:2043-2048)
;   * one clean of the 512-byte scratch block - 8 lines and a barrier
;   * this file's pre-pass civac over the destination rectangle
;   * four device-register writes and a barrier
;   * a poll loop on CS, every read of which is an uncached round trip
;     over the same fabric the engine is using (dma.pi4:1397-1403)
;   * this file's post-pass civac, if readback is on
;
; SO THE DEFAULT IS CHOSEN TO BE SAFE RATHER THAN OPTIMAL. 4096 pixels
; is a 64 x 64 block. Below it sit every glyph cell (8 x 16 = 128), every
; caret, every one-pixel rule and every short DisplayHLine - which is to
; say the whole text console's hot path, which stays exactly as fast as
; it is today. Above it sit window clears, panel fills and full-surface
; clears, which are the cases where even a modest engine wins and where
; the CPU is measurably terrible.
;
; TURNING THE ENGINE ON THEREFORE CANNOT MAKE THE CONSOLE SLOWER, and
; that is the property the default is protecting. It is deliberately not
; the property "extract the most speed", because the number needed to
; chase that does not exist yet.
;
; WHEN pi4DmaProbe HAS RUN: it prints the CPU and the DMA time for the
; same fill. Divide the DMA path's fixed overhead by 148 ns and put the
; answer here, with the bench date beside it. Zero is legal and means
; "always use the engine", which is the right setting for a measurement
; sweep and the wrong one for a console.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DisplayDmaMin(px.i)
  If px < 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  dsp_dmaMin = px
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DisplayDmaMinGet()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_dmaMin
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE COUNTERS, AND WHY A SILENT FALLBACK WOULD BE THE WORST OUTCOME
;
; Every accelerated primitive here can decline: the rectangle is too
; small, the depth is not 32bpp, the engine is not ready, the geometry
; does not fit a register field, the destination is outside the window
; DmaSetBounds() declared. In every one of those cases it falls back to
; the CPU and DRAWS THE RIGHT PICTURE - which is exactly what makes it
; dangerous. A program can run for months believing it is accelerated
; while every single call declines, and the only symptom is a frame time
; nobody compared against anything.
;
;   DisplayDmaOps()        transfers the engine actually performed
;   DisplayDmaFallbacks()  calls that chose the CPU by policy
;   DisplayDmaRefusals()   calls where the engine was ASKED and said no
;
; The third is the one to watch. A non-zero refusal count with the engine
; switched on means dma.pi4 turned something away, and DmaErrorText()
; will say what - most likely #DMA_ERR_BOUNDS because DisplayDmaBind()
; was never called.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DisplayDmaOps()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_dmaOps
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DisplayDmaFallbacks()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_dmaBack
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DisplayDmaRefusals()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_dmaFail
EndProcedure

Procedure DisplayDmaStatsReset()
  dsp_dmaOps  = 0
  dsp_dmaBack = 0
  dsp_dmaFail = 0
EndProcedure

CompilerIf #DSP_DMA_LINKED = 1

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayDmaBind - point the engine's write window at this surface.
;
; DmaSetBounds() is MANDATORY in dma.pi4 and every transfer is refused
; until it has been called (dma.pi4:1082-1101). It takes an address and
; a length, and getting either wrong is the one fault on this board whose
; symptom is a machine that stops answering rather than a bad picture.
; This is the one line that cannot be got wrong, because both numbers
; come from the firmware's own answer.
;
; IT DOES NOT CALL DmaInit(). Bringing the engine up is the main
; program's business - it chooses the scratch block, and it may want to
; ask the firmware which channels are free first (dma.pi4:222-230) - and
; a display library that quietly claimed a DMA channel would be doing
; something no caller asked for and nothing here could undo.
;
; THE ORDER IS: DisplayInit, DmaSetScratch, DisplayDmaBind, DmaInit,
; DisplayUseDma(1). DisplayDmaBind before DmaInit because DmaInit
; refuses until the bounds are declared.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DisplayDmaBind()
  If dsp_ready = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn DmaSetBounds(dsp_base, dsp_size)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DspDmaReady - may an accelerated primitive even be attempted?
;
; Internal, and it is only the CHEAP half of the decision - the half
; that does not depend on the rectangle. The per-rectangle half lives in
; each primitive because the answers differ.
;
; DEPTH IS CHECKED HERE AND IT IS A HARD 32bpp GATE. 16bpp is
; deliberately NOT routed through the engine, and this is a capability
; gap rather than an oversight, so it is stated rather than implied:
; DmaFillPattern32 repeats a 32-BIT word, so a 5:6:5 fill needs the
; half-word doubled into both halves and needs every row to start on an
; even byte, and neither of those is exercised by a single one of the 91
; cases pi4DmaGate.pi4 runs - every pattern in that gate is a 32-bit
; word and every oracle byte is (j mod 4) of it. Routing an unexercised
; path through a bus master is precisely the shape of thing this tree
; does not do. To add it: double the half-word, require
; (dsp_pitch & 1) = 0, and add a 16bpp group to the gate FIRST.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DspDmaReady()
  If dsp_ready = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If dsp_dmaOn = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If dsp_bpp <> 4
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If DmaReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DspDmaSyncRect - THE ONE PLACE THE CACHE DISCIPLINE LIVES.
;
; Clean AND invalidate every line the rectangle touches, with ONE
; barrier for the whole rectangle. It is called on both sides of every
; transfer - before, for hazards A and C; after, for hazard B - and it
; is one procedure so that there is one place for the operation to be
; wrong rather than six.
;
; civac AND NOT cvac. DisplayCacheLine() above is a plain clean, which is
; right when the only other reader is the display controller: the line
; may stay resident because nobody else will write those bytes. Here
; somebody else WILL write those bytes, so the line must go. dma.pi4's
; DmaCacheRect() is the civac, and using it rather than growing a second
; cache-maintenance implementation here is the point - one encoding, one
; barrier rule, one place to audit. dma.pi4:762-798 works through why it
; is civac and not cvac and not ivac.
;
; ROW BY ROW THROUGH DmaCacheRect, which collapses to a single
; contiguous walk when the rectangle happens to be full-width with no
; padding. That is the same special case DspFlushSpan carves out and it
; matters for the same reason: the console's scroll block IS contiguous
; on this board, and walking it as 1080 separate rows would pay 1080
; barriers for one region.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DspDmaSyncRect(x0.i, y0.i, w.i, h.i)
  Define a.i
  If w < 1
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If h < 1
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  a = (dsp_base + (y0 * dsp_pitch)) + (x0 * dsp_bpp)
  DmaCacheRect(a, dsp_pitch, w * dsp_bpp, h)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DspDmaFill - a clipped rectangle fill through the engine.
;
; Returns 1 if the ENGINE DREW IT and the caller must do nothing more -
; in particular must NOT call DspTouched. Returns 0 if the caller must
; fall back to the CPU path, which is always a legal thing to do and is
; always the same picture.
;
; THE CLIP IS DUPLICATED FROM DspFillCell RATHER THAN SHARED, and that
; is worth one sentence because it looks like waste. Sharing it would
; mean returning four clipped values, and this dialect has no way to
; return four; the alternative - four globals written by one procedure
; and read by another - is a much worse thing to have on a path that a
; bus master's destination address is computed from. Two eight-line
; clips that can be read side by side beat one clip and four globals.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DspDmaFill(x.i, y.i, w.i, h.i, colour.i)
  Define x0.i
  Define y0.i
  Define x1.i
  Define y1.i
  Define dst.i
  Define rows.i
  Define rowBytes.i

  If DspDmaReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  x0 = x
  y0 = y
  x1 = x + w
  y1 = y + h
  If x0 < 0 : x0 = 0 : EndIf
  If y0 < 0 : y0 = 0 : EndIf
  If x1 > dsp_w : x1 = dsp_w : EndIf
  If y1 > dsp_h : y1 = dsp_h : EndIf
  If x1 <= x0 Or y1 <= y0
    ; Nothing to draw. Returning 1 - "handled" - is correct and is what
    ; the CPU path does with the same rectangle: it draws nothing and
    ; returns. Returning 0 here would send an empty rectangle down the
    ; CPU path to be clipped away a second time, and would count a
    ; fallback that did not happen.
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ; The policy threshold. Counted as a fallback, because that is what it
  ; is: a deliberate decision not to use the engine, and one a bench
  ; needs to be able to see the size of.
  If ((x1 - x0) * (y1 - y0)) < dsp_dmaMin
    dsp_dmaBack = dsp_dmaBack + 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  rowBytes = (x1 - x0) * dsp_bpp
  rows     = y1 - y0
  dst      = (dsp_base + (y0 * dsp_pitch)) + (x0 * dsp_bpp)

  ; --- 1. hazards A and C, before the engine starts ------------------
  DspDmaSyncRect(x0, y0, x1 - x0, rows)

  ; --- 2. the transfer -----------------------------------------------
  If DmaFillPattern32(dst, dsp_pitch, rowBytes, rows, colour & $FFFFFFFF) = 0
    ; THE ENGINE WAS ASKED AND SAID NO. Counted separately from a policy
    ; fallback because the two mean completely different things: this
    ; one has a reason, DmaErrorText() has it in words, and a program
    ; seeing it should go and read it rather than shrug.
    ;
    ; The pre-pass above is not undone and does not need to be. It left
    ; the rectangle clean and absent from the D-cache, which is a
    ; perfectly ordinary state for cacheable memory; the CPU path that
    ; follows will simply refill as it writes.
    dsp_dmaFail = dsp_dmaFail + 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; --- 3. hazard B, after it finished --------------------------------
  If dsp_dmaRead = 1
    DspDmaSyncRect(x0, y0, x1 - x0, rows)
  EndIf

  ; --- 4. deliberately NO DspTouched ---------------------------------
  dsp_dmaOps = dsp_dmaOps + 1
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DspDmaCopyRect - a clipped surface-to-surface copy through the engine.
;
; Same contract as DspDmaFill: 1 means done, 0 means fall back.
;
; THE OVERLAP RULES ARE THE ENGINE'S AND THEY ARE NARROWER THAN THE CPU
; PATH'S, so this declines rather than reproducing them. DmaCopy2D
; permits an overlap only when the destination is BELOW the source in
; memory - the downward scroll - and refuses the other direction
; (dma.pi4:2113-2127). DisplayCopyRect's CPU loop handles both, by
; choosing its row order. Rather than duplicate that reasoning here and
; get it subtly different, anything that is not plainly safe is handed
; back to the loop that already knows how to do it.
;
; ONE SYNC OVER THE UNION, NOT TWO OVER THE HALVES. The source must be
; cleaned (hazard A) and the destination must be cleaned and invalidated
; (hazard C); civac does both, and for a scroll the two rectangles
; overlap, so a single pass over the union is fewer operations AND
; removes the question of what happens to a cache line that belongs to
; both. The union is computed in ROWS AND COLUMNS, not in addresses,
; because a rectangle is not contiguous and an address-range union would
; sweep the full width of every row in between.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DspDmaCopyRect(sx.i, sy.i, dx.i, dy.i, w.i, h.i)
  Define ux0.i
  Define uy0.i
  Define ux1.i
  Define uy1.i
  Define s.i
  Define d.i

  If DspDmaReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If w < 1 Or h < 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If sx < 0 Or sy < 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If dx < 0 Or dy < 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; No clipping here, unlike the fill. DisplayCopyRect clips BOTH the
  ; source and the destination against each other and against the
  ; surface before it decides anything, so by the time this is called
  ; the rectangle already fits; re-deriving that would be a second
  ; chance to get it wrong.
  If (sx + w) > dsp_w Or (dx + w) > dsp_w
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (sy + h) > dsp_h Or (dy + h) > dsp_h
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  If (w * h) < dsp_dmaMin
    dsp_dmaBack = dsp_dmaBack + 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; THE ONLY OVERLAP THE ENGINE IS ALLOWED. Destination strictly above
  ; the source in rows, or the same rows with no horizontal shift at
  ; all. Everything else - a downward-on-screen move, a sideways move
  ; within a row, anything the addresses could interleave - goes to the
  ; CPU.
  If dy > sy
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If dy = sy And dx <> sx
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  s = (dsp_base + (sy * dsp_pitch)) + (sx * dsp_bpp)
  d = (dsp_base + (dy * dsp_pitch)) + (dx * dsp_bpp)

  ; The union, in surface coordinates.
  ux0 = sx
  If dx < ux0 : ux0 = dx : EndIf
  uy0 = sy
  If dy < uy0 : uy0 = dy : EndIf
  ux1 = sx + w
  If (dx + w) > ux1 : ux1 = dx + w : EndIf
  uy1 = sy + h
  If (dy + h) > uy1 : uy1 = dy + h : EndIf

  DspDmaSyncRect(ux0, uy0, ux1 - ux0, uy1 - uy0)

  If DmaCopy2D(d, dsp_pitch, s, dsp_pitch, w * dsp_bpp, h) = 0
    dsp_dmaFail = dsp_dmaFail + 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  If dsp_dmaRead = 1
    DspDmaSyncRect(dx, dy, w, h)
  EndIf

  dsp_dmaOps = dsp_dmaOps + 1
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DspDmaSpanUp - the console scroll's contiguous block, in ONE transfer.
;
; dst is BELOW src and the whole block is one run of bytes, which is the
; case DisplayScroll already detects and DspCopyUp already exploits. Here
; it means TDMODE can stay clear and the whole 6.39 MB becomes a single
; linear control block with no stride for the engine to re-evaluate per
; row - dma.pi4's DmaCopy (:2189-2207) carves out the same special case
; on the same condition and with the same warning attached.
;
; The sync is over the union, which for two overlapping contiguous runs
; is one contiguous range from dst to the end of src - so this is one
; DmaCacheRange and one barrier over the lot, not a row walk.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DspDmaSpanUp(dst.i, src.i, bytes.i)
  If DspDmaReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If bytes < 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If dst >= src
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (bytes / dsp_bpp) < dsp_dmaMin
    dsp_dmaBack = dsp_dmaBack + 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  DmaCacheRange(dst, (src - dst) + bytes)

  If DmaCopy(dst, src, bytes) = 0
    dsp_dmaFail = dsp_dmaFail + 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  If dsp_dmaRead = 1
    DmaCacheRange(dst, bytes)
  EndIf

  dsp_dmaOps = dsp_dmaOps + 1
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

CompilerEndIf


; ======================================================================
;  THE SURFACE
;
;  EVERY row address in this section is
;
;      dsp_base + y * dsp_pitch + x * dsp_bpp
;
;  and there is no other way to reach a pixel in this file. See PITCH IS
;  NOT WIDTH * BYTES-PER-PIXEL.
;
;  EVERY primitive CLIPS to the surface. An unclipped write here is not
;  a graphical glitch - it is a store into whatever DRAM happens to
;  follow the framebuffer, which on this board could be the monitor, a
;  payload, or U-Boot.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DspRowFill32 / DspRowFill16 - w pixels of one colour starting at byte
; address a. Two procedures rather than one with a test inside, so the
; per-pixel branch is out of the inner loop. Callers have already
; clipped and have already checked the depth.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHY THESE LOOPS ARE UNROLLED, AND WHY THAT IS NOT PREMATURE
; ======================================================================
; MEASURED, 2026-08-26, out of Monitor/anvil.img.asm - the listing
; the compiler emitted for the image that was actually on the board:
;
;   DspRowFill32's one-pixel body, __L454..__L455 at
;   anvil.img.asm:9364-9411, is THIRTY instructions long. Exactly
;   ONE of them ("str w12, [x13]") writes a pixel.
;
; The other twenty-nine are the backend's calling convention showing
; through. Procedure locals on this target are statically allocated -
; there is no per-call frame, which is written up at DisplayPutC() -
; so every local lives at a fixed address and every mention of one
; costs "adrp / add / ldr" to read or "adrp / add / str" to write.
; "p = p + 4" is eight instructions. The loop test is seven. None of
; that is wasted work the compiler could have removed; it is the shape
; of the target, and the only way to spend less of it per byte is to
; move more bytes per trip round the loop.
;
; So the body writes THIRTY-TWO bytes per iteration instead of four.
; That takes the instruction cost from 7.5 per byte to about 1.4, and
; on the 1920 x 1080 console it is the difference between a scroll you
; watch and a scroll you do not.
;
; PokeI, not four PokeLs. PokeI is POINTER WIDTH and pointer width is
; EIGHT bytes on this target - the same fact gpio.pi4:64-66 records as
; a trap (a PeekI of GPLEV0 spans two registers) is what makes it the
; right tool here. The colour is doubled into both halves once, before
; the loop, so the wide store writes two identical pixels.
;
; THE DOUBLING IS MASKED. colour arrives as a .i and PokeL only ever
; used its low 32 bits, so a caller that passed a negative number, or
; anything with rubbish in the top half, was harmless before and must
; stay harmless now: "(colour & $FFFFFFFF) << 32" would otherwise smear
; that rubbish across the odd pixels only, which is the kind of fault
; that looks like a memory error rather than a masking error.
;
; ALIGNMENT. An eight-byte store needs an eight-byte-aligned address,
; and "a" is only guaranteed four-byte aligned: it is
; dsp_base + row * dsp_pitch + x0 * 4, and x0 is a pixel column the
; caller chose. The head pixel is therefore written singly when the
; address is odd-in-words, which also makes the run eight-aligned for
; the rest of the loop. dsp_base is $100-aligned (the allocate tag asks
; for #MBX_FB_ALLOC_ALIGN) and the pitch the firmware returned is 7680,
; so on this board it is the x0 term alone that decides, but none of
; that is assumed here.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DspRowFill32(a.i, w.i, colour.i)
  Define p.i
  Define n.i
  Define left.i
  Define wide.i

  If w < 1
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  p = a
  left = w
  colour = colour & $FFFFFFFF
  wide = colour | (colour << 32)

  ; Head: at most one pixel, to reach an eight-byte boundary.
  If (p & 7) <> 0
    PokeL(p, colour)
    p = p + 4
    left = left - 1
  EndIf

  ; Body: eight pixels - thirty-two bytes - per trip.
  n = left >> 3
  While n > 0
    PokeI(p, wide)
    PokeI(p + 8, wide)
    PokeI(p + 16, wide)
    PokeI(p + 24, wide)
    p = p + 32
    n = n - 1
  Wend
  left = left & 7

  ; Tail: the last seven pixels or fewer, one at a time. A pair loop
  ; here would save three stores and cost a second loop to get wrong.
  While left > 0
    PokeL(p, colour)
    p = p + 4
    left = left - 1
  Wend
EndProcedure

Procedure DspRowFill16(a.i, w.i, colour.i)
  Define p.i
  Define n.i
  p = a
  n = 0
  While n < w
    PokeW(p, colour)
    p = p + 2
    n = n + 1
  Wend
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DspFillCell - the clipped fill, WITHOUT the auto-flush.
;
; Internal. It exists because the glyph renderer emits several runs per
; glyph row and flushes the whole cell once at the end: routing those
; runs through DisplayFillRect would clean the same cache lines up to
; eight times per row, for no benefit, on every character printed.
; DisplayFillRect is this plus the flush.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DspFillCell(x.i, y.i, w.i, h.i, colour.i)
  Define x0.i
  Define y0.i
  Define x1.i
  Define y1.i
  Define row.i
  Define a.i
  Define n.i

  If dsp_ready = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  x0 = x
  y0 = y
  x1 = x + w
  y1 = y + h
  If x0 < 0 : x0 = 0 : EndIf
  If y0 < 0 : y0 = 0 : EndIf
  If x1 > dsp_w : x1 = dsp_w : EndIf
  If y1 > dsp_h : y1 = dsp_h : EndIf
  If x1 <= x0 Or y1 <= y0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  n = x1 - x0
  row = y0
  If dsp_bpp = 4
    While row < y1
      a = (dsp_base + (row * dsp_pitch)) + (x0 * 4)
      DspRowFill32(a, n, colour)
      row = row + 1
    Wend
  Else
    While row < y1
      a = (dsp_base + (row * dsp_pitch)) + (x0 * 2)
      DspRowFill16(a, n, colour)
      row = row + 1
    Wend
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayFillRect - a FILLED rectangle. This is the primitive
; font.pico2's Tft796Rect maps onto, and the one FontTextField() uses to
; stop a shrinking number ghosting over its former self.
;
; The unclipped arguments are handed on to the flush, which clips them
; again itself - cheaper than returning the clipped rectangle out of the
; fill, and it keeps the fill with one job.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE DMA BRANCH IS HERE AND NOT IN DspFillCell, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE
; POINT OF THE SPLIT.
;
; DspFillCell is what the glyph renderer calls, several times per glyph
; row, for runs of a few pixels. Sending those through a bus master
; would be absurd - a submission costs more than the pixels - and worse,
; it would put a device poll inside the character loop.
;
; DisplayFillRect is what a UI calls for a rectangle it means as a
; rectangle. That is the call worth accelerating, and it is also the one
; that must NOT flush afterwards when the engine drew it: DspDmaFill
; returning 1 means the pixels are already in DRAM and DspTouched would
; be 130,000 wasted operations at best. See step 4 of the sequence in
; CACHE COHERENCY WITH A BUS MASTER.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DisplayFillRect(x.i, y.i, w.i, h.i, colour.i)
  CompilerIf #DSP_DMA_LINKED = 1
    If DspDmaFill(x, y, w, h, colour) = 1
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
  CompilerEndIf
  DspFillCell(x, y, w, h, colour)
  DspTouched(x, y, w, h)
EndProcedure

Procedure DisplayClear(colour.i)
  DisplayFillRect(0, 0, dsp_w, dsp_h, colour)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayHLine - w pixels of one colour along a row.
;
; THIS IS THE FONT ENGINE'S RUN PRIMITIVE. font.pico2's transparent path
; opens a one-row window and calls Tft796Push(colour, count); that is
; exactly this call, and it is the reason it exists as its own name
; rather than as DisplayFillRect with h = 1.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DisplayHLine(x.i, y.i, w.i, colour.i)
  DisplayFillRect(x, y, w, 1, colour)
EndProcedure

Procedure DisplayVLine(x.i, y.i, h.i, colour.i)
  DisplayFillRect(x, y, 1, h, colour)
EndProcedure

; DisplayFrameRect - a one-pixel outline. Four fills; the corners are
; written twice, which costs four pixels and keeps the arithmetic
; obviously right.
Procedure DisplayFrameRect(x.i, y.i, w.i, h.i, colour.i)
  If w < 1 Or h < 1
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  DisplayFillRect(x, y, w, 1, colour)
  DisplayFillRect(x, y + h - 1, w, 1, colour)
  DisplayFillRect(x, y, 1, h, colour)
  DisplayFillRect(x + w - 1, y, 1, h, colour)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayPixel - one pixel, clipped.
;
; PokeL at 32bpp and PokeW at 16bpp, never a bare Poke: a bare one is
; eight bytes on this target and would write two pixels, running off the
; end of the last row of the surface.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DisplayPixel(x.i, y.i, colour.i)
  Define a.i
  If dsp_ready = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If x < 0 Or y < 0 Or x >= dsp_w Or y >= dsp_h
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  a = (dsp_base + (y * dsp_pitch)) + (x * dsp_bpp)
  If dsp_bpp = 4
    PokeL(a, colour)
  Else
    PokeW(a, colour)
  EndIf
  DspTouched(x, y, 1, 1)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayGetPixel - read one back, or -1 outside the surface.
;
; A framebuffer CAN be read; the ST7796 cannot, and font.pico2 has a
; comment built on that limitation. Masked with $FFFFFFFF because PeekL
; sign-extends and a pixel with the top alpha byte at $FF has bit 31
; set, so an unmasked read of an opaque white pixel comes back negative.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DisplayGetPixel(x.i, y.i)
  Define a.i
  If dsp_ready = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If x < 0 Or y < 0 Or x >= dsp_w Or y >= dsp_h
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  a = (dsp_base + (y * dsp_pitch)) + (x * dsp_bpp)
  If dsp_bpp = 4
    ProcedureReturn PeekN(a) & $FFFFFFFF
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn PeekW(a) & $FFFF
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayBlit - copy a rectangle of ALREADY-NATIVE-FORMAT pixels from
; memory into the surface.
;
; srcPitch is in BYTES and is the source's own row stride, which is a
; separate number from the surface's pitch for exactly the reason the
; surface has one: a source rectangle is usually a window into a larger
; image. Passing w*bpp for srcPitch is correct only for a tightly packed
; source, and the argument exists so that is a decision rather than an
; assumption.
;
; NO FORMAT CONVERSION. The source must already be in the surface's
; depth and channel order. This is the primitive an image loader or a
; future off-screen buffer sits on, and putting a converter inside it
; would make every caller pay for one.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DisplayBlit(*src, srcPitch.i, x.i, y.i, w.i, h.i)
  Define row.i
  Define col.i
  Define s.i
  Define d.i
  Define sx0.i
  Define sy0.i
  Define x0.i
  Define y0.i
  Define x1.i
  Define y1.i

  If dsp_ready = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  ; Clip against the surface, and carry the clip back into the source so
  ; a rectangle that hangs off the left or top edge copies its visible
  ; part rather than its first w columns.
  x0 = x
  y0 = y
  x1 = x + w
  y1 = y + h
  If x0 < 0 : x0 = 0 : EndIf
  If y0 < 0 : y0 = 0 : EndIf
  If x1 > dsp_w : x1 = dsp_w : EndIf
  If y1 > dsp_h : y1 = dsp_h : EndIf
  If x1 <= x0 Or y1 <= y0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  sx0 = x0 - x
  sy0 = y0 - y

  row = 0
  While row < (y1 - y0)
    s = (*src + ((sy0 + row) * srcPitch)) + (sx0 * dsp_bpp)
    d = (dsp_base + ((y0 + row) * dsp_pitch)) + (x0 * dsp_bpp)
    col = 0
    If dsp_bpp = 4
      While col < (x1 - x0)
        PokeL(d, PeekN(s))
        s = s + 4
        d = d + 4
        col = col + 1
      Wend
    Else
      While col < (x1 - x0)
        PokeW(d, PeekU(s))
        s = s + 2
        d = d + 2
        col = col + 1
      Wend
    EndIf
    row = row + 1
  Wend

  DspTouched(x0, y0, x1 - x0, y1 - y0)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayCopyRect - surface to surface. This is what the console's
; scroll is built on.
;
; THE ROW ORDER IS CHOSEN FROM THE OVERLAP, and it has to be: scrolling
; up copies a region onto itself shifted, and a forward copy would read
; rows it has already overwritten. Destination above source -> copy
; forwards. Destination below -> copy backwards. Same row -> the
; horizontal case, which the per-pixel loop below still gets wrong if
; the columns overlap, so that one is refused rather than silently
; smeared.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DisplayCopyRect(sx.i, sy.i, dx.i, dy.i, w.i, h.i)
  Define row.i
  Define col.i
  Define s.i
  Define d.i
  Define cw.i
  Define chh.i

  If dsp_ready = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If w < 1 Or h < 1
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If sx < 0 Or sy < 0 Or dx < 0 Or dy < 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  ; Clip the rectangle so that BOTH the source and the destination fit.
  cw = w
  chh = h
  If sx + cw > dsp_w : cw = dsp_w - sx : EndIf
  If dx + cw > dsp_w : cw = dsp_w - dx : EndIf
  If sy + chh > dsp_h : chh = dsp_h - sy : EndIf
  If dy + chh > dsp_h : chh = dsp_h - dy : EndIf
  If cw < 1 Or chh < 1
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  ; The one overlapping case this loop cannot do: same rows, columns
  ; overlapping. Refused rather than smeared - a partial answer that
  ; looks like corruption is worse than none.
  If sy = dy And sx <> dx
    If dx > sx And dx < (sx + cw)
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
    If sx > dx And sx < (dx + cw)
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
  EndIf

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; THE ENGINE GETS THE CLIPPED RECTANGLE, NOT THE CALLER'S.
  ;
  ; Everything above has already reconciled the source and the
  ; destination against each other and against the surface, and has
  ; already turned away the one overlap neither path can do. Handing
  ; DspDmaCopyRect the raw arguments would make it redo all of that and
  ; would give it a second chance to disagree - and a disagreement here
  ; is a bus master writing somewhere the CPU path would not have.
  ;
  ; It declines whatever it is not certain of, and declining costs one
  ; comparison and the loop below runs exactly as it did before.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  CompilerIf #DSP_DMA_LINKED = 1
    If DspDmaCopyRect(sx, sy, dx, dy, cw, chh) = 1
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
  CompilerEndIf

  If dy <= sy
    row = 0
    While row < chh
      s = (dsp_base + ((sy + row) * dsp_pitch)) + (sx * dsp_bpp)
      d = (dsp_base + ((dy + row) * dsp_pitch)) + (dx * dsp_bpp)
      col = 0
      If dsp_bpp = 4
        While col < cw
          PokeL(d, PeekN(s))
          s = s + 4
          d = d + 4
          col = col + 1
        Wend
      Else
        While col < cw
          PokeW(d, PeekU(s))
          s = s + 2
          d = d + 2
          col = col + 1
        Wend
      EndIf
      row = row + 1
    Wend
  Else
    row = chh - 1
    While row >= 0
      s = (dsp_base + ((sy + row) * dsp_pitch)) + (sx * dsp_bpp)
      d = (dsp_base + ((dy + row) * dsp_pitch)) + (dx * dsp_bpp)
      col = 0
      If dsp_bpp = 4
        While col < cw
          PokeL(d, PeekN(s))
          s = s + 4
          d = d + 4
          col = col + 1
        Wend
      Else
        While col < cw
          PokeW(d, PeekU(s))
          s = s + 2
          d = d + 2
          col = col + 1
        Wend
      EndIf
      row = row - 1
    Wend
  EndIf

  DspTouched(dx, dy, cw, chh)
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  TEXT - a fixed-width character cell renderer
;
;  The glyph format is font.pico2's, so a font generated by
;  tools/make_font.py reads correctly here: 1 bit per pixel, MSB of the
;  first byte is the LEFTMOST pixel, rows padded to a whole number of
;  bytes. What is NOT font.pico2's is the width table - this renderer is
;  fixed-width, so glyph n starts at n * cellH * rowBytes and there is
;  no per-glyph offset to look up. A proportional font needs the real
;  engine; see the header.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayFontUse - select a font by the ADDRESS of its bitmap blob.
;
; By address, not by name, so replacing the font never touches the
; renderer:  DisplayFontUse(?my_glyphs, 8, 16, 32, 126)
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DisplayFontUse(bitsAddr.i, cellW.i, cellH.i, first.i, last.i)
  If cellW < 1 Or cellH < 1 Or last < first
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  dsp_fbits  = bitsAddr
  dsp_fw     = cellW
  dsp_fh     = cellH
  dsp_frb    = (cellW + 7) / 8
  dsp_ffirst = first
  dsp_flast  = last
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure DisplayFontDefault()
  DisplayFontUse(?dsp_font8x16, 8, 16, 32, 126)
EndProcedure

; DisplayScale - magnify the font by a whole number. Each glyph pixel
; becomes an n by n block. Whole numbers only: anything else needs
; filtering, and a filtered 1-bit glyph is a blurred glyph.
Procedure.i DisplayScale(n.i)
  If n < 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  dsp_scale = n
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; The cell size actually drawn, scale included. The console's geometry
; is computed from these and so should a caller's layout be.
Procedure.i DisplayCharWidth()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_fw * dsp_scale
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DisplayCharHeight()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_fh * dsp_scale
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayChar - one character, OPAQUE. Returns the advance in pixels, or
; 0 if the character is not in the font's range.
;
; DRAWN AS HORIZONTAL RUNS, not pixel by pixel. Every column of the cell
; is written either way - that is what opaque means - but an 8-wide
; glyph row is typically two to four runs of the same colour rather than
; eight separate pixels, and each run is one DspRowFill call instead of
; eight. At scale > 1 the saving multiplies with the scale.
;
; The bit test is  b & (128 >> (col & 7))  - MSB first, which is the
; order the format stores and the order a shift-and-test loop consumes
; naturally. Parenthesised because & binds tighter than >>.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DisplayChar(x.i, y.i, ch.i, fg.i, bg.i)
  Define r.i
  Define col.i
  Define b.i
  Define ink.i
  Define runInk.i
  Define runStart.i
  Define gbase.i
  Define rowbase.i
  Define py.i
  Define sc.i
  Define colour.i
  Define p.i

  If dsp_ready = 0 Or dsp_fbits = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If ch < dsp_ffirst Or ch > dsp_flast
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  sc = dsp_scale
  gbase = dsp_fbits + (((ch - dsp_ffirst) * dsp_fh) * dsp_frb)

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  THE 8 x N, SCALE 1, 32bpp FAST PATH
  ;
  ;  This is the console's shape and nothing else's: the built-in font
  ;  is 8 x 16 (one byte per glyph row, dsp_frb = 1), DisplayScale is 1,
  ;  the surface is 32bpp, and the cell is wholly inside it. Every one
  ;  of those is TESTED; the run-length path below is unchanged and
  ;  still handles scaled text, 16bpp, wider fonts and clipped cells.
  ;
  ;  WHY IT IS FASTER THAN THE RUN PATH, WHICH IS NOT THE OBVIOUS
  ;  ANSWER. The run path is a good algorithm - an 8-wide glyph row is
  ;  two to four runs, not eight pixels - and on a machine where a local
  ;  variable lives in a register it would win. It does not win here.
  ;  Procedure locals on this backend are statically allocated, so the
  ;  eight-iteration COLUMN SCAN that finds the runs costs about thirty
  ;  instructions an iteration, and each run it finds is then a CALL to
  ;  DspFillCell which re-derives the clip and the row address from
  ;  globals - roughly 150 instructions - to write two or three pixels
  ;  through DspRowFill32. Per glyph that came to something like eleven
  ;  thousand instructions to place 128 pixels.
  ;
  ;  Writing the eight pixels straight out, unrolled, with the row
  ;  address computed once, is about 3,000. The run detection was buying
  ;  fewer STORES at the price of far more instructions, and on this
  ;  target stores are the cheap half.
  ;
  ;  THE BIT ORDER IS THE FORMAT'S: MSB of the byte is the LEFTMOST
  ;  pixel, so the tests walk 128, 64, 32 ... 1. Written out rather than
  ;  looped because a loop is the thing being removed.
  ;
  ;  PeekB SIGN-EXTENDS on this target, hence the & $FF - the same trap
  ;  the run path documents below.
  ; ==================================================================
  If sc = 1 And dsp_fw = 8 And dsp_frb = 1 And dsp_bpp = 4
    If x >= 0 And y >= 0 And (x + 8) <= dsp_w And (y + dsp_fh) <= dsp_h
      r = 0
      While r < dsp_fh
        b = PeekB(gbase + r) & $FF
        p = (dsp_base + ((y + r) * dsp_pitch)) + (x * 4)
        If (b & 128) <> 0 : PokeL(p,      fg) : Else : PokeL(p,      bg) : EndIf
        If (b &  64) <> 0 : PokeL(p +  4, fg) : Else : PokeL(p +  4, bg) : EndIf
        If (b &  32) <> 0 : PokeL(p +  8, fg) : Else : PokeL(p +  8, bg) : EndIf
        If (b &  16) <> 0 : PokeL(p + 12, fg) : Else : PokeL(p + 12, bg) : EndIf
        If (b &   8) <> 0 : PokeL(p + 16, fg) : Else : PokeL(p + 16, bg) : EndIf
        If (b &   4) <> 0 : PokeL(p + 20, fg) : Else : PokeL(p + 20, bg) : EndIf
        If (b &   2) <> 0 : PokeL(p + 24, fg) : Else : PokeL(p + 24, bg) : EndIf
        If (b &   1) <> 0 : PokeL(p + 28, fg) : Else : PokeL(p + 28, bg) : EndIf
        r = r + 1
      Wend
      DspTouched(x, y, 8, dsp_fh)
      ProcedureReturn 8
    EndIf
  EndIf

  ; The whole cell is about to be covered, so flush it once at the end
  ; rather than once per run.
  r = 0
  While r < dsp_fh
    rowbase = gbase + (r * dsp_frb)
    py = y + (r * sc)
    runStart = 0
    ; The run open at column 0. PeekB sign-extends on this target, so
    ; the & $FF is not decoration.
    runInk = 0
    b = PeekB(rowbase) & $FF
    If (b & 128) <> 0
      runInk = 1
    EndIf
    col = 1
    ; col = dsp_fw is a SENTINEL pass: ink is set to 2, which differs
    ; from both 0 and 1, so the last run is always closed and emitted
    ; without a copy of the emit code after the loop.
    While col <= dsp_fw
      ink = 2
      If col < dsp_fw
        ink = 0
        b = PeekB(rowbase + (col >> 3)) & $FF
        If (b & (128 >> (col & 7))) <> 0
          ink = 1
        EndIf
      EndIf
      If ink <> runInk
        If runInk = 1
          colour = fg
        Else
          colour = bg
        EndIf
        DspFillCell(x + (runStart * sc), py, (col - runStart) * sc, sc, colour)
        runStart = col
        runInk = ink
      EndIf
      col = col + 1
    Wend
    r = r + 1
  Wend

  DspTouched(x, y, dsp_fw * sc, dsp_fh * sc)
  ProcedureReturn dsp_fw * sc
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayCharClear - one character, TRANSPARENT: ink pixels only,
; whatever is underneath left alone. Returns the advance.
;
; This is the mode that puts text over a picture. Opaque text over a
; picture stamps a rectangle of background colour around every letter.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DisplayCharClear(x.i, y.i, ch.i, fg.i)
  Define r.i
  Define col.i
  Define b.i
  Define ink.i
  Define inRun.i
  Define runStart.i
  Define gbase.i
  Define rowbase.i
  Define py.i
  Define sc.i

  If dsp_ready = 0 Or dsp_fbits = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If ch < dsp_ffirst Or ch > dsp_flast
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  sc = dsp_scale
  gbase = dsp_fbits + (((ch - dsp_ffirst) * dsp_fh) * dsp_frb)

  r = 0
  While r < dsp_fh
    rowbase = gbase + (r * dsp_frb)
    py = y + (r * sc)
    inRun = 0
    runStart = 0
    col = 0
    While col <= dsp_fw
      ink = 0
      If col < dsp_fw
        b = PeekB(rowbase + (col >> 3)) & $FF
        If (b & (128 >> (col & 7))) <> 0
          ink = 1
        EndIf
      EndIf
      If ink = 1 And inRun = 0
        inRun = 1
        runStart = col
      ElseIf ink = 0 And inRun = 1
        DspFillCell(x + (runStart * sc), py, (col - runStart) * sc, sc, fg)
        inRun = 0
      EndIf
      col = col + 1
    Wend
    r = r + 1
  Wend

  DspTouched(x, y, dsp_fw * sc, dsp_fh * sc)
  ProcedureReturn dsp_fw * sc
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DisplayText(x.i, y.i, *s, fg.i, bg.i)
  Define i.i
  Define c.i
  Define cx.i
  Define adv.i
  i = 0
  cx = x
  Repeat
    c = PeekB(*s + i) & $FF
    If c = 0
      Break
    EndIf
    adv = DisplayChar(cx, y, c, fg, bg)
    If adv > 0
      cx = cx + adv
    EndIf
    i = i + 1
  ForEver
  ProcedureReturn cx - x
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DisplayTextClear(x.i, y.i, *s, fg.i)
  Define i.i
  Define c.i
  Define cx.i
  Define adv.i
  i = 0
  cx = x
  Repeat
    c = PeekB(*s + i) & $FF
    If c = 0
      Break
    EndIf
    adv = DisplayCharClear(cx, y, c, fg)
    If adv > 0
      cx = cx + adv
    EndIf
    i = i + 1
  ForEver
  ProcedureReturn cx - x
EndProcedure

; DisplayTextWidth - how wide a string will be, before drawing it.
; Fixed width, so this is a count and a multiply; it exists so callers
; can centre text without special-casing the font, and so the signature
; survives a swap to a proportional engine.
Procedure.i DisplayTextWidth(*s)
  Define i.i
  Define c.i
  Define n.i
  i = 0
  n = 0
  Repeat
    c = PeekB(*s + i) & $FF
    If c = 0
      Break
    EndIf
    If c >= dsp_ffirst And c <= dsp_flast
      n = n + 1
    EndIf
    i = i + 1
  ForEver
  ProcedureReturn (n * dsp_fw) * dsp_scale
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  THE CONSOLE
;
;  A rectangle of the surface, measured in character cells, with a
;  cursor and a scroll. This is the "text now" half of the brief: the
;  thing a bring-up program uses to print a line and see it.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayConsole - place the console window, in PIXELS, and recompute
; its size in cells. Call it again after DisplayScale() or
; DisplayFontUse(), because the cell size has changed underneath it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DisplayConsole(x.i, y.i, w.i, h.i)
  Define cwid.i
  Define chgt.i
  If dsp_ready = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  cwid = DisplayCharWidth()
  chgt = DisplayCharHeight()
  If cwid < 1 Or chgt < 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  dsp_cox = x
  dsp_coy = y
  dsp_cw = w
  dsp_ch = h
  dsp_cols = w / cwid
  dsp_rows = h / chgt
  dsp_cx = 0
  dsp_cy = 0
  If dsp_cols < 1 Or dsp_rows < 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DisplayConsoleFull()
  ProcedureReturn DisplayConsole(0, 0, dsp_w, dsp_h)
EndProcedure

Procedure DisplayColour(fg.i, bg.i)
  dsp_fg = fg
  dsp_bg = bg
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DisplayCols()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_cols
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DisplayRows()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_rows
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DisplayCursorCol()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_cx
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DisplayCursorRow()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_cy
EndProcedure

Procedure DisplayLocate(col.i, row.i)
  If col < 0 : col = 0 : EndIf
  If row < 0 : row = 0 : EndIf
  If col >= dsp_cols : col = dsp_cols - 1 : EndIf
  If row >= dsp_rows : row = dsp_rows - 1 : EndIf
  dsp_cx = col
  dsp_cy = row
EndProcedure

; DisplayCls - clear the console window only, not the whole surface, and
; home the cursor. The distinction matters once anything else is drawn
; on the screen beside the console.
Procedure DisplayCls()
  DisplayFillRect(dsp_cox, dsp_coy, dsp_cols * DisplayCharWidth(), dsp_rows * DisplayCharHeight(), dsp_bg)
  dsp_cx = 0
  dsp_cy = 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DspCopyUp - move n bytes from src to dst where dst is BELOW src in
; memory, forwards, eight bytes at a time.
;
; Internal, and deliberately narrow: FORWARDS ONLY. That is safe for
; overlapping regions when dst < src and for nothing else, which is
; precisely the scroll-up case and is the same restriction, for the
; same reason, that DisplayCopyRect's dy <= sy branch carries. A
; general memmove belongs in memory.pi4, and a library does not include
; a library (house rule 10), so this stays here, small, and used by one
; caller.
;
; THE UNROLL IS THE WHOLE POINT and the measurement is in the header of
; DspRowFill32. DisplayCopyRect's one-pixel body, __L597..__L598 at
; Monitor/anvil.img.asm:11477-11519, is FORTY-THREE instructions
; long and exactly two of them - "ldr w11, [x12]" and "str w11, [x13]" -
; move the pixel. Scrolling this console one text row moves 1,546,752
; pixels, so that loop alone was sixty-six MILLION instructions per
; scrolled line. It was never the bus. It was the loop.
;
; Sixty-four bytes per trip: one Cortex-A72 cache line, which also means
; the clean that follows has exactly one line's worth of dirty data per
; iteration to write back, and eight-byte stores below that.
;
; ALIGNMENT IS CHECKED, NOT ASSUMED. src and dst here differ by a whole
; number of pitches, so they always share their low three bits, but the
; PAIR can still be four-byte aligned rather than eight (dsp_base plus a
; pitch that is a multiple of four but not of eight). Three tiers -
; eight, four, one - so every case is correct and only a genuinely
; byte-aligned surface, which this firmware has never produced, falls to
; the slow one.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DspCopyUp(dst.i, src.i, n.i)
  Define s.i
  Define d.i
  Define k.i
  Define left.i

  If n < 1
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  s = src
  d = dst
  left = n

  ; Eight-byte tier. "(d | s) & 7" - both have to be aligned, and the
  ; OR tests both in one go.
  If ((d | s) & 7) = 0
    k = left >> 6
    While k > 0
      PokeI(d,      PeekI(s))
      PokeI(d + 8,  PeekI(s + 8))
      PokeI(d + 16, PeekI(s + 16))
      PokeI(d + 24, PeekI(s + 24))
      PokeI(d + 32, PeekI(s + 32))
      PokeI(d + 40, PeekI(s + 40))
      PokeI(d + 48, PeekI(s + 48))
      PokeI(d + 56, PeekI(s + 56))
      s = s + 64
      d = d + 64
      k = k - 1
    Wend
    left = left & 63
    While left >= 8
      PokeI(d, PeekI(s))
      s = s + 8
      d = d + 8
      left = left - 8
    Wend
  ElseIf ((d | s) & 3) = 0
    ; Four-byte tier. PeekN is the 32-bit read; PokeL the 32-bit write.
    k = left >> 5
    While k > 0
      PokeL(d,      PeekN(s))
      PokeL(d + 4,  PeekN(s + 4))
      PokeL(d + 8,  PeekN(s + 8))
      PokeL(d + 12, PeekN(s + 12))
      PokeL(d + 16, PeekN(s + 16))
      PokeL(d + 20, PeekN(s + 20))
      PokeL(d + 24, PeekN(s + 24))
      PokeL(d + 28, PeekN(s + 28))
      s = s + 32
      d = d + 32
      k = k - 1
    Wend
    left = left & 31
    While left >= 4
      PokeL(d, PeekN(s))
      s = s + 4
      d = d + 4
      left = left - 4
    Wend
  EndIf

  ; Whatever is left, including the whole run on a byte-aligned
  ; surface. PeekA is the UNSIGNED byte read - PeekB sign-extends on
  ; this target, and a pixel byte above $7F would be written back as
  ; $FFFFFF80-something.
  While left > 0
    PokeA(d, PeekA(s))
    s = s + 1
    d = d + 1
    left = left - 1
  Wend
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayScroll - move the console contents up by n rows of cells and
; fill the vacated rows with the background colour.
;
; It used to be one call to DisplayCopyRect with the destination ABOVE
; the source - the direction that copy handles forwards, see the overlap
; note there - and that is still what happens for a console that is not
; the full width of the surface. On a display that could not be read
; back this would be impossible without a shadow buffer; it is the
; clearest thing a framebuffer buys over the SPI panel the font engine
; grew up on.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHY THERE IS A SECOND PATH: THE CONSOLE'S ROWS ARE USUALLY CONTIGUOUS
; ======================================================================
; A framebuffer row is w * bpp bytes of picture followed by however much
; padding the pitch adds. When the console starts at column 0 AND the
; row it spans is exactly the pitch, there is no padding between one
; console row and the next - the whole block being scrolled is ONE run
; of bytes, and it can be copied and cleaned as one, with no per-row
; address arithmetic and ONE barrier instead of 848.
;
; That is the case on this board: 1920 x 1080 at 32bpp comes back with a
; pitch of 7680, which is exactly 1920 * 4, and the monitor's console is
; the full width. (It was 1824 x 984, pitch 7296, until disable_overscan=1
; went into config.txt on 2026-08-26 - the identity held under both, which
; is the point of testing it rather than hardcoding it.) It is NOT a case this file may assume - a mode with
; padding is legal and the header says so at length - so it is TESTED,
; and the general path is kept underneath rather than deleted.
;
; DisplayCopyRect is deliberately NOT the thing being made faster. It is
; a public primitive with clipping and overlap rules of its own that
; other code depends on, and this file is not the only reader of it
; today. The console gets its own engine; the primitive keeps its
; semantics.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DisplayScroll(rows.i)
  Define chgt.i
  Define cwid.i
  Define keep.i
  Define px.i
  Define bytes.i
  Define top.i
  Define srcA.i
  Define dstA.i
  Define didCpu.i

  If dsp_ready = 0 Or rows < 1
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  chgt = DisplayCharHeight()
  cwid = DisplayCharWidth()
  px = dsp_cols * cwid
  If rows >= dsp_rows
    DisplayCls()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  keep = (dsp_rows - rows) * chgt

  ; The contiguous case. Three conditions, all of them cheap:
  ;   - the console starts at the left edge of the surface,
  ;   - the row it spans IS the pitch, so there is no padding, and
  ;   - the block it is about to touch is inside the surface, which the
  ;     general path would have clipped and this one will not.
  top = dsp_coy
  If dsp_cox = 0 And (px * dsp_bpp) = dsp_pitch And top >= 0 And (top + (dsp_rows * chgt)) <= dsp_h
    bytes = keep * dsp_pitch
    dstA  = dsp_base + (top * dsp_pitch)
    srcA  = dstA + (rows * chgt * dsp_pitch)

    ; ----------------------------------------------------------------
    ; THE ACCELERATED SCROLL, AND WHY IT IS TWO TRANSFERS AND NOT ONE.
    ;
    ; The move and the fill are separate operations on adjacent regions
    ; and the engine has no way to express them as one control block -
    ; a chain would, and dma.pi4 deliberately does not chain yet
    ; (:298-301). Two submissions is the honest cost and it is still one
    ; per 6.39 MB rather than one per row.
    ;
    ; EITHER MAY DECLINE INDEPENDENTLY and the CPU picks up whichever
    ; did. That is why the two are tested separately instead of being
    ; wrapped in one "did the engine do the scroll" flag: a fill that
    ; falls back after a copy that did not must still clean its own
    ; vacated rows, and a single flag would have lost that.
    ;
    ; THE AUTO-FLUSH BELOW IS THEN CONDITIONAL ON THE CPU HAVING DONE
    ; SOMETHING. DspFlushSpan over the whole console block after a
    ; transfer the engine performed would clean lines this file has just
    ; deliberately invalidated - harmless today, and exactly the write
    ; that would put a stale line back over a correct transfer if the
    ; pre-pass were ever dropped. Hazard C in the header.
    ;
    ; THE ONE PLACE THE MIXED CASE IS SUBTLE, worked through here so it
    ; is not rediscovered: when the copy falls back to the CPU and the
    ; fill does not, the CPU leaves dirty lines over the KEPT rows while
    ; the engine writes the VACATED rows immediately below them. Those
    ; two regions meet at a row boundary, and a row boundary is only
    ; cache-line aligned if the pitch happens to be a multiple of 64 -
    ; 7680 is, on this board, and a padded mode need not be. So one
    ; 64-byte line can hold dirty CPU bytes from the copy AND bytes the
    ; engine is about to write.
    ;
    ; It is safe, and it is safe because of the OPERATION and not by
    ; luck: DspDmaFill's pre-pass is "dc civac", which WRITES THE DIRTY
    ; HALF BACK before dropping the line. A plain invalidate there would
    ; discard the copy's last partial line and lose a strip of the
    ; scrolled picture on exactly the modes where the pitch is padded.
    ; dma.pi4:762-798 is where that choice is argued; this is the case
    ; that would have punished the other one.
    ; ----------------------------------------------------------------
    didCpu = 0

    CompilerIf #DSP_DMA_LINKED = 1
      If DspDmaSpanUp(dstA, srcA, bytes) = 0
        DspCopyUp(dstA, srcA, bytes)
        didCpu = 1
      EndIf
      If DspDmaFill(0, top + keep, px, rows * chgt, dsp_bg) = 0
        DspFillCell(0, top + keep, px, rows * chgt, dsp_bg)
        didCpu = 1
      EndIf
    CompilerElse
      DspCopyUp(dstA, srcA, bytes)
      ; The vacated rows, then ONE clean over the copy and the fill
      ; together - they are adjacent, so cleaning them separately would
      ; pay two barriers for one contiguous region.
      DspFillCell(0, top + keep, px, rows * chgt, dsp_bg)
      didCpu = 1
    CompilerEndIf

    If didCpu = 1
      If dsp_auto = 1
        DspFlushSpan(dstA, dsp_rows * chgt * dsp_pitch)
      EndIf
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  ; The general path. Both of these already carry their own DMA branch,
  ; so a console that is not full-width is accelerated too - through
  ; DisplayCopyRect's and DisplayFillRect's own decisions rather than
  ; through a third copy of them here.
  DisplayCopyRect(dsp_cox, dsp_coy + (rows * chgt), dsp_cox, dsp_coy, px, keep)
  DisplayFillRect(dsp_cox, dsp_coy + keep, px, rows * chgt, dsp_bg)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayScrollChunk - how many rows a console that has run off the
; bottom scrolls at once. 1 restores line-at-a-time scrolling exactly.
;
; ======================================================================
; JUMP SCROLL, AND WHY THE DEFAULT IS NOT 1
; ======================================================================
; Scrolling one text row on the monitor's console - 1920 x 1080, pitch
; 7680, 240 cols x 60 rows below a 120-pixel banner - copies
; 59 * 16 * 7680 = 7.25 MB. Scrolling EIGHT rows at once copies
; 52 * 16 * 7680 = 6.39 MB: twelve per cent less work to advance eight
; times as far. Per line printed that is 799 KB instead of 7.25 MB, a
; factor of NINE, and it costs nothing but arithmetic.
;
; WHAT IT COSTS THE READER, honestly: the text jumps by eight rows
; instead of gliding by one, and the bottom seven rows of the console
; are blank between jumps - so the last line printed before a prompt can
; sit seven rows above the bottom edge. This is DEC's "jump scroll"
; against its "smooth scroll" and it is what every fast text console has
; done since serial terminals; it reads as quick rather than as broken.
; The FINAL state of the screen is identical either way.
;
; IT IS A KNOB BECAUSE IT IS A JUDGEMENT. A caller who wants the old
; behaviour writes DisplayScrollChunk(1) and gets it back exactly, at
; the old cost. The value is clamped rather than refused: a chunk at or
; above the console height would scroll the whole thing away, which
; DisplayScroll() correctly turns into a clear, and a console that clears
; itself every screenful is not what anyone asking for a big chunk meant.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DisplayScrollChunk(n.i)
  If n < 1
    n = 1
  EndIf
  dsp_schunk = n
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DisplayScrollChunkGet()
  ProcedureReturn dsp_schunk
EndProcedure

Procedure DisplayNewLine()
  Define k.i
  dsp_cx = 0
  dsp_cy = dsp_cy + 1
  If dsp_cy >= dsp_rows
    ; Clamped HERE and not in the setter, because dsp_rows changes under
    ; the console - DisplayConsole(), DisplayScale() and DisplayFontUse()
    ; all move it - and a value clamped when it was set would still be
    ; too big after the console shrank. Half the height is the ceiling:
    ; beyond that the reader loses more context per jump than the jump
    ; saves in copying.
    k = dsp_schunk
    If k < 1
      k = 1
    EndIf
    If k > (dsp_rows / 2)
      k = dsp_rows / 2
    EndIf
    If k < 1
      k = 1
    EndIf
    DisplayScroll(k)
    dsp_cy = dsp_rows - k
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisplayPutC - one character at the cursor, with the four control codes
; a console actually needs.
;
;   13  CR         carriage return, cursor to column 0, no scroll
;   10  LF         newline, scrolling if it was the last row
;    8  BS         back one column, and ERASE - a backspace that leaves
;                  the character on the glass is not a backspace
;    9  TAB        forward to the next multiple of 8 columns
;
; CR and LF are handled SEPARATELY rather than as a pair. A stream from
; a serial line sends CRLF and would otherwise scroll twice; a stream
; from a program that sends bare LF would otherwise never return to
; column 0. Treating CR as "column 0" and LF as "next line" makes both
; correct and needs no state.
;
; THE TAB EXPANSION GOES THROUGH DspPutGlyph, NOT BACK THROUGH
; DisplayPutC, and that is not a style preference. Procedure locals are
; statically allocated on this backend - there is no per-call frame - so
; a procedure that calls itself overwrites its own arguments and the
; outer call resumes with the inner one's values. The compiler refuses
; it outright, and that refusal is how the first draft of this procedure
; was caught: DisplayPutC(9) emitted its spaces with DisplayPutC(32).
; Factoring the printable-character path out is the fix, and it is also
; the only shape that would have been correct if the compiler had let it
; through.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; DspPutGlyph - a printable character at the cursor, then advance and
; wrap. Internal, and deliberately calls nothing that can call it back.
Procedure DspPutGlyph(c.i)
  Define cwid.i
  Define chgt.i
  cwid = DisplayCharWidth()
  chgt = DisplayCharHeight()
  DisplayChar(dsp_cox + (dsp_cx * cwid), dsp_coy + (dsp_cy * chgt), c, dsp_fg, dsp_bg)
  dsp_cx = dsp_cx + 1
  If dsp_cx >= dsp_cols
    DisplayNewLine()
  EndIf
EndProcedure

Procedure DisplayPutC(ch.i)
  Define c.i
  Define n.i
  Define cwid.i
  Define chgt.i

  If dsp_ready = 0 Or dsp_cols < 1
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  c = ch & $FF

  If c = 13
    dsp_cx = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If c = 10
    DisplayNewLine()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If c = 8
    If dsp_cx > 0
      dsp_cx = dsp_cx - 1
      cwid = DisplayCharWidth()
      chgt = DisplayCharHeight()
      DisplayFillRect(dsp_cox + (dsp_cx * cwid), dsp_coy + (dsp_cy * chgt), cwid, chgt, dsp_bg)
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If c = 9
    n = 8 - (dsp_cx % 8)
    While n > 0
      DspPutGlyph(32)
      n = n - 1
    Wend
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If c < 32
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  DspPutGlyph(c)
EndProcedure

Procedure DisplayPrint(*s)
  Define i.i
  Define c.i
  i = 0
  Repeat
    c = PeekB(*s + i) & $FF
    If c = 0
      Break
    EndIf
    DisplayPutC(c)
    i = i + 1
  ForEver
EndProcedure

Procedure DisplayPrintLn(*s)
  DisplayPrint(*s)
  DisplayNewLine()
EndProcedure

; DisplayPrintDec / DisplayPrintHex - the two number printers a bring-up
; screen needs immediately. Without them the first thing every caller
; writes is one of these, and the first thing it gets wrong is the
; 64-bit width.
;
; Nineteen digits, because .i is 64 bits on this target and a ten-digit
; scale would silently print only the bottom ten digits of a large
; value. The same reasoning and the same constant as anvil.pi4's
; PutDec().
Procedure DisplayPrintDec(v.i)
  Define d.i
  Define scale.i
  Define started.i
  If v < 0
    DisplayPutC(45)
    v = 0 - v
  EndIf
  If v = 0
    DisplayPutC(48)
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  scale = 1000000000000000000
  started = 0
  While scale > 0
    d = (v / scale) % 10
    If d <> 0 Or started <> 0
      DisplayPutC(48 + d)
      started = 1
    EndIf
    scale = scale / 10
  Wend
EndProcedure

; MASK AFTER THE SHIFT. >> is arithmetic on a signed .i, but the sign
; only ever fills bits above the nibble being printed, so & 15 makes it
; irrelevant and a negative value prints as its two's complement rather
; than as a minus sign - which for an address is what you want.
Procedure DisplayPrintHex(v.i, digits.i)
  Define k.i
  Define n.i
  k = (digits - 1) * 4
  While k >= 0
    n = (v >> k) & 15
    If n < 10
      DisplayPutC(48 + n)
    Else
      DisplayPutC(55 + n)
    EndIf
    k = k - 4
  Wend
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  THE BUILT-IN FONT - 8 x 16, ASCII 32..126, 95 glyphs, 1520 bytes.
;
;  DejaVu Sans Mono at 13 px rasterised into an 8x16 cell, one bit per
;  pixel, MSB of each byte is the LEFTMOST pixel, 16 consecutive bytes
;  per glyph (one per row) because the cell is 8 wide and (8+7)/8 = 1.
;  DejaVu is the same family the project's existing font_sans16 tables
;  come from, and its licence permits this.
;
;  The comment at the end of each line is the character, so the table is
;  checkable by eye against the code it claims to encode. GENERATED, not
;  hand-written; regenerate rather than patch a glyph.
;
;  Reached with ?dsp_font8x16 and PeekB, never with Read/Restore - see
;  the note in the header about the open Read-stride bug.
; ======================================================================

DataSection
  dsp_font8x16:
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; ' '  32   space
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$18,$18,$18,$18,$18,$18,$00,$18,$18,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; '!'  33
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$24,$24,$24,$24,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; '"'  34
  Data.a $00,$00,$12,$16,$14,$7F,$24,$24,$FE,$68,$48,$48,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; '#'  35
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$00,$3C,$68,$40,$38,$1C,$02,$46,$3C,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; '$'  36
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$70,$90,$90,$76,$18,$6E,$0B,$0B,$0E,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; '%'  37
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$3C,$60,$20,$30,$59,$CB,$C6,$46,$3A,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; '&'  38
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$18,$18,$18,$18,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; '''  39
  Data.a $00,$08,$08,$18,$10,$10,$10,$10,$10,$10,$18,$08,$08,$00,$00,$00   ; '('  40
  Data.a $00,$30,$10,$18,$18,$08,$08,$08,$08,$08,$18,$10,$30,$00,$00,$00   ; ')'  41
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$10,$52,$38,$38,$52,$10,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; '*'  42
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$00,$18,$18,$18,$FE,$18,$18,$18,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; '+'  43
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$18,$18,$10,$10,$00,$00   ; ','  44
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$3C,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; '-'  45
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$18,$18,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; '.'  46
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$06,$04,$0C,$08,$08,$10,$10,$30,$20,$60,$40,$00,$00   ; '/'  47
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$3C,$64,$46,$42,$5A,$42,$46,$64,$3C,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; '0'  48
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$78,$08,$08,$08,$08,$08,$08,$08,$3E,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; '1'  49
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$3C,$44,$06,$04,$0C,$18,$30,$60,$7E,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; '2'  50
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$3C,$44,$06,$04,$3C,$06,$06,$46,$3C,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; '3'  51
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$0C,$1C,$14,$24,$64,$44,$7E,$04,$04,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; '4'  52
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$7C,$60,$60,$7C,$04,$06,$06,$44,$38,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; '5'  53
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$3C,$60,$40,$7C,$66,$42,$42,$66,$3C,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; '6'  54
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$7E,$06,$04,$0C,$08,$18,$18,$10,$30,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; '7'  55
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$3C,$66,$46,$64,$3C,$66,$42,$66,$3C,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; '8'  56
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$3C,$64,$46,$46,$66,$3E,$06,$04,$38,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; '9'  57
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$18,$18,$00,$00,$00,$18,$18,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; ':'  58
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$18,$18,$00,$00,$00,$18,$18,$10,$10,$00,$00   ; ';'  59
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$02,$1C,$60,$60,$1C,$02,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; '<'  60
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$FE,$00,$00,$FE,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; '='  61
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$40,$38,$0E,$0E,$38,$40,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; '>'  62
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$3C,$06,$06,$0C,$18,$10,$00,$10,$10,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; '?'  63
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$3C,$62,$42,$CF,$93,$93,$93,$CF,$40,$60,$1C,$00,$00   ; '@'  64
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$18,$18,$3C,$2C,$24,$66,$7E,$42,$C3,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; 'A'  65
  Data.a $00,$00,$00,$7C,$46,$46,$46,$7C,$46,$42,$46,$7C,$00,$00,$00,$00   ; 'B'  66
